; Fibers (self-scaling stage 1): context-switch primitives (milestone 1.1),
; Fiber regions (milestone 1.2), and the round-robin scheduler
; (milestone 1.3).
;
; Context switch: vendored assembly under fiber/ (fcontext_arm64.S AAPCS64,
; fcontext_x86_64.S SysV — the two ABIs covering all four LP64 targets),
; Boost.Context fcontext style: a context is a stack pointer to a frame of
; callee-saved registers. tools/fcontext.sh assembles the host's file with
; clang; every libscaly.a build archives the object alongside libscaly.o,
; and the seed/compiler links carry it too (the Fiber procedures below
; reference the switcher). API (no transfer_t struct return): the resumed
; side receives the JUMPER's fresh context in the first argument register —
; a fresh context's entry function gets it as its argument, a suspended
; context gets it as scaly_jump_context's return value. An entry function
; must never return (it traps).
;
; Fiber regions (milestone 1.2): a Fiber owns an mmap'd stack (guard page
; at the bottom, no growing in v1) and is its OWN REGION ROOT — it starts
; with empty StackBucket allocator state (stack_head/stack_top), so the
; first function-local page allocated inside the fiber creates a fresh
; bucket chain belonging to the fiber. resume/yield/finish swap that
; per-fiber allocator state with the resuming side's at EVERY context
; crossing (the Fiber record always holds the non-running side's context +
; allocator state). The HeapBucket allocator (heap_head) is shared by
; all fibers of one thread (thread_local since milestone 2.2): it is
; bitmap-managed, not LIFO, so the fibers of a thread can interleave
; extension/exclusive-page traffic safely. dispose
; frees the fiber's whole region wholesale: every root page still on its
; stack (extension + exclusive chains included), the stack buckets
; themselves, then the mmap'd stack.
;
; The API lives as statics on the Fiber concept (Fiber.create / resume /
; started / yield / finish / dispose): user programs resolve cross-package
; concept statics reliably, unlike free functions with concept-pointer
; parameters (planner gap, 2026-07-08).
;
; NOTE: SCALY_TRACE_ROOT reports a fiber's entry procedure as unbalanced
; by design — its root pages are not popped one by one; the region dies
; wholesale in Fiber.dispose.
;
; Entry protocol (milestone 1.2 — the raw Fiber layer):
;   procedure my_fiber(first_ctx: pointer[void])
;   {
;       Fiber.started first_ctx    ; hand the resumer's context to the record
;       ...work; Fiber.yield() as often as needed...
;       Fiber.finish()             ; never returns
;   }
;   let f Fiber.create(page, &my_fiber)
;   while Fiber.resume(f) { ... }
;   let freed Fiber.dispose(f)
;
; Scheduler (milestone 1.3): a round-robin dispatcher over a FIFO ready
; queue that wraps the started/finish entry protocol away — a task is a
; plain ZERO-ARGUMENT procedure. The trampoline trick: a delegate value
; is a pointer to a closure record whose slot 0 is the thunk, so a
; SchedTask record with the task's function pointer as its FIRST field
; is itself a callable delegate value; scheduler_entry casts the running
; task to TaskFn and calls the user procedure indirectly. Task records,
; fibers, and the queue all live on the scheduler's host page (they must
; survive the spawning fiber's region teardown). Fiber.resume is only
; ever called from the main context (Scheduler.run / a main-context
; join); a task that joins blocks on the target's waiter chain and
; yields back to the dispatcher — no nested resumes. A finished task's
; whole fiber region is disposed exactly once, eagerly by the
; dispatcher (see milestone 1.6 below).
;
;   procedure my_task()
;   {
;       ...work; Scheduler.yield() to give up the slice...
;       ...Scheduler.spawn(&other) / Scheduler.join(t) as needed...
;   }
;   let s Scheduler.create(page)
;   let t Scheduler.spawn(&my_task)
;   Scheduler.run()                ; until the ready queue drains
;   let freed Scheduler.join(t)    ; reap: dispose the fiber's region
;
; Channels (milestone 1.4): channels with region-ownership transfer. A
; message is an opaque pointer to an object graph rooted on its OWN
; HeapBucket page (Page.allocate_page) — heap pages are process-global,
; not part of any fiber's region, so a message survives the sending
; fiber's wholesale teardown by construction. send TRANSFERS ownership:
; after send the sender must not touch the message again (the escape
; checker's use-after-send rule enforces this); the receiver frees the
; message region when done (deallocate_extensions + Page.release_page
; on Page.get of the message).
;
; Milestone 1.4 shipped this as a single-thread RENDEZVOUS type
; (blocking send over the scheduler's park-and-wake mechanics);
; milestone 2.4 added the cross-thread async Mailbox beside it. The
; post-stage-3 unification retired the rendezvous implementation:
; Channel IS the async ownership channel — sends never block, a
; sender-handle count closes it, receive returns null when it is
; closed and drained. See the Channel concept header below for the
; full contract and usage.
;
; Evented I/O (milestone 1.5): suspending read/write over a per-scheduler
; readiness poller. The backend is a small C shim (fiber/eio.c, kqueue on
; darwin / epoll on linux, selected by the preprocessor; tools/eio.sh
; compiles it into eio.o beside fcontext.o) — it cannot live in Scaly
; code because the committed seed ships ONE scaly.ll for all four
; targets, and because two calls need C: fcntl is variadic (a
; fixed-prototype extern silently drops the third argument on arm64),
; and errno is a thread-local macro (the shim maps EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK
; to -2). Io.read/Io.write try the nonblocking syscall and, on
; would-block, arm the fd (oneshot) and park the running task; the
; dispatcher blocks in the poller exactly when the ready queue is empty
; but I/O waiters exist (Scheduler.step_or_wait), and wakes each task
; whose readiness arrived. Exit 106 keeps its meaning: ready queue
; empty, NO I/O waiters, tasks still blocked. From the main context an
; Io call arms the fd with a null tag and drives the dispatcher until
; its own event fires. At most one parked task per fd (the epoll
; backend keeps one entry per fd).
;
;   Io.set_nonblocking(fd)           ; fd: i32, once per fd
;   let n Io.read(fd, buf, count)    ; suspends; 0 = EOF, -1 = hard error
;   let n Io.write(fd, buf, count)   ; suspends; partial writes possible
;
; TCP accept path (milestone 1.5 follow-up): localhost TCP sockets and a
; suspending accept. Socket setup lives in the shim too — sockaddr_in's
; layout is OS-specific (darwin leads with sin_len, linux with a 16-bit
; family), so Scaly code cannot fill one portably — and accept needs the
; shim's errno mapping (would-block -> -2). Io.accept parks exactly like
; Io.read: readiness on a listening fd means a connection is pending.
; connect performs a blocking handshake (instant against a live local
; listener); the accepted/connected fd stays blocking until
; Io.set_nonblocking.
;
;   let lfd Io.listen(port)          ; 0 = ephemeral; -1 = failure
;   let port Io.local_port(lfd)      ; the kernel's port assignment
;   let cfd Io.accept(lfd)           ; suspends; -1 = hard error
;   let fd Io.connect(port)          ; -1 = failure
;
; The 10k demo (milestone 1.6): fiber-per-connection servers need two
; final pieces. spawn_arg passes one pointer-sized argument to a task
; (an accepted fd, cast through size_t) — the task reads it back with
; Scheduler.task_arg. And the dispatcher reaps eagerly: the moment a
; task finishes, Scheduler.step disposes its whole fiber region (stack,
; buckets, pages) — a connection handler's region dies WITH the
; connection, so 10k sequentially-completing handlers keep a flat
; memory peak instead of 10k live regions awaiting a join. join still
; blocks until the task finished; reaping stays idempotent (a later
; join returns 0 pages).
;
;   let t Scheduler.spawn_arg(&handler, (fd as size_t) as pointer[void])
;   ; inside handler:
;   let fd (Scheduler.task_arg() as size_t) as i32
;
; Stage-2 warm-up (milestone 2.0): stack pooling, sized spawns, and
; overflow diagnostics. Fiber.dispose returns a DEFAULT-SIZED stack to a
; process-global pool instead of munmapping it; Fiber.create reuses a
; pooled stack (guard page already in place — no mmap, no guard call), so
; sequential fiber churn stops paying two syscalls plus VMA bookkeeping
; per fiber. Custom-sized stacks (Scheduler.spawn_sized for tasks known
; to run flat or deep) bypass the pool and munmap as before. The pool
; holds the high-water mark of CONCURRENT fibers; Fiber.drain_stack_pool
; hands everything back to the OS. And the first Fiber.create installs a
; SIGSEGV/SIGBUS handler (shim-owned: sigaltstack/sigaction layouts are
; OS-specific) whose classifier recognizes a fault inside the RUNNING
; fiber's guard page — a fiber stack overflow dies with a message and
; exit 108 instead of an anonymous signal; every other fault re-crashes
; with the default action, exactly as without the handler.
;
; Thread primitive (milestone 2.1): pthread_create/pthread_join as
; DIRECT externs — fixed prototypes, no variadics, no errno, and
; pthread_t is a pointer-sized value on every LP64 target (a pointer on
; darwin, unsigned long on linux glibc), carried as size_t. Per the shim
; containment rule none of this needs C. Thread.spawn runs a Scaly
; procedure on a second OS thread; Thread.join waits for it. The entry
; signature is `procedure entry(arg: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]`
; (take it with &entry; return null — the result is discarded).
;
; Thread-local allocator state (milestone 2.2): every mutable module
; global — heap_head, stack_head/stack_top, root-page scratch, the
; fiber globals below — is thread_local (the Emitter marks all mutable
; globals so), giving each OS thread its own allocator, scheduler slot,
; and stack pool. The milestone-2.1 no-allocation convention is lifted:
; a thread entry may allocate freely — pages, Strings, containers,
; fibers, schedulers, channels — all on its private state
; (tests/fiber/thread_alloc.scaly is the demonstrator).
;
; Freelist safety (milestone 2.3): CROSS-THREAD page hand-off is safe —
; a per-bucket spinlock (root_pages.scaly bucket_lock, built on the
; Emitter's atomic primitives) serializes the bitmap and the
; chain-membership transitions, and a thread that frees into a full
; (hence orphaned) bucket ADOPTS it into its own chain. A page may be
; allocated on one thread and released on another
; (tests/fiber/thread_churn.scaly). Still per-thread: schedulers and
; fibers; the Channel (milestone 2.4, below) crosses threads.
;
;   let t Thread.spawn(&entry, arg)   ; arg: pointer[void], handed to entry
;   Thread.join(t)                    ; blocks until entry returned
;
; Cross-thread channels (milestone 2.4): the ASYNC ownership channel
; connecting fibers/threads across OS threads — shipped beside the
; rendezvous type as Mailbox, THE Channel since the unification (see
; the Channel concept header for the full contract). Unbounded sends
; never block; a bounded channel (create_bounded) applies backpressure
; — a full send waits for a receiver to free a slot. A sender-handle
; count closes the channel when the last sender retires, waking parked
; receivers with null — deadlock avoidance by acyclic channel topology
; instead of detection. Infra:
; SchedTask.home (the spawning scheduler), a per-scheduler MPSC inject
; queue (CAS Treiber stack, drained at every step) and a wake handle in
; the eio shim (EVFILT_USER/eventfd) that remote senders poke. exit 106
; is unchanged for programs without channel parks.
;
; Colorless stdlib I/O (milestone 2.5): the same Console/File call
; blocks on the main context, suspends inside a task, and needs no
; scheduler at all — suspension is a runtime property, not a type
; color. Three legs:
;
; 1. No-scheduler fallback: an Io call on a thread WITHOUT a scheduler
;    waits for readiness in poll(2) instead of parking (struct pollfd
;    and the POLLIN/POLLOUT values are layout-uniform across all four
;    LP64 targets, so poll is a direct extern per the containment
;    rule). Io no longer requires a scheduler.
; 2. Console writes through Io.write_all (loops over partial writes):
;    plain write on a blocking stdout, suspending inside a task once
;    the fd is nonblocking.
; 3. File I/O offloads to worker threads: readiness pollers are useless
;    for REGULAR FILES (always "ready", reads still block on disk), so
;    Io.offload ships a blocking operation — a `procedure fn(arg:
;    pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]` — to a worker thread and
;    parks the requesting task; the worker wakes it through the
;    milestone-2.4 inject queue + wake handle. On the main context (or
;    without a scheduler) the operation simply runs in place. The pool
;    is per REQUESTING thread (the pool pointer is a thread-local
;    global; no Scaly mutable global is process-wide since milestone
;    2.2), grows lazily up to IO_POOL_WORKERS, and its workers block in
;    a 1-byte pipe read between items (one byte written per queued
;    item). An offloaded operation runs on ANOTHER THREAD: it must not
;    read thread-local globals of the requester; results either travel
;    on their own fresh heap page (channel-message style) or are
;    written into requester-owned memory named in the request cell —
;    the requester is parked, its region quiescent, so such writes are
;    race-free (File's read leg freads straight into the String's
;    buffer this way, zero copies). At thread exit the trampoline
;    drains the pool (Io.drain_pool: pipe write end closed -> workers
;    read 0 and exit -> joined; pool page freed) and closes the
;    scheduler's poller + wake handle (Io.close_poller); both are also
;    callable directly — the next use lazily rebuilds.
;
;   let res Io.offload(&blocking_op, arg)   ; parks in a task; runs
;                                           ; in place otherwise
;
; Work-stealing task pool (stage-3 milestone 3.2): the task runtime the
; promoted-for emission (milestone 3.3/4.4) dispatches
; to, usable directly today. A pool is N OS threads (Thread.spawn),
; each owning a spinlocked work deque; TaskPool.submit places a task on
; a deque round-robin and a worker whose own deque runs dry STEALS from
; the others'. The wake channel is the IoPool pattern: one pipe byte
; per queued item — a worker blocks in a 1-byte read and, having
; consumed a byte, is entitled to exactly one item SOMEWHERE (pushes
; precede byte writes, takes follow byte reads), so its steal scan
; always terminates. Closing the write end (TaskPool.shutdown) drains
; the remaining bytes first, then hands every worker a 0 — queued work
; still runs before the workers exit and are joined.
;
; One region per task: the task entry (`procedure fn(arg: pointer[void])
; returns pointer[void]`, run via scaly_call_ptr) executes on a worker
; thread — its allocations root on that worker's thread-local allocator
; and unwind with the entry frame, so each task's region dies with the
; task. Results land in CALLER-OWNED DISJOINT SLOTS (Vector.put at the
; task's own index is the canonical target): the submitter is parked in
; TaskGroup.wait while tasks run, its region is quiescent, so those
; cross-thread writes are race-free — the milestone-2.5 zero-copy
; discipline. Anything a task allocates and keeps beyond its return
; must ride its own heap page, channel-message style.
;
; A TaskGroup counts in-flight tasks (atomic) and carries its own pipe:
; the completion that takes pending to zero writes one byte, and wait
; re-checks the counter after every byte it reads — stale bytes from
; intermediate zero-crossings (submit waves reusing one group) are
; harmless. Work items ride the GROUP'S own heap page (bump-allocated
; by the submitter, freed wholesale in dispose), so the pool needs no
; allocator and nothing to track. Contracts: one submitting thread per
; group, wait only after every submit of the wave, dispose only after
; wait, shutdown only when no group has work in flight; wait blocks the
; calling THREAD (call it from the main context, not inside a scheduled
; task). No shared global is needed anywhere: the pool record travels
; by pointer (spawn cells, task arguments), channel-style.
;
;   let pool TaskPool.create(4 as i64)
;   let g TaskGroup.create()
;   ...TaskPool.submit(pool, &task_fn, arg_i, g) per task...
;   TaskGroup.wait(g)                 ; blocks until every task finished
;   TaskGroup.dispose(g)
;   TaskPool.shutdown(pool)           ; drain queued work, join workers
;
; Exit codes: 103 mmap, 104 stack guard, 105 munmap, 106 scheduler
; deadlock (ready queue empty, no I/O waiters, tasks still blocked in
; a join), 107 poller failure (create/arm/wait or
; set_nonblocking), 108 fiber stack overflow (guard page hit), 109
; thread spawn/join failure, 110 send on a closed channel, 111 worker
; pool failure (pipe creation), 112 task pool/group pipe failure.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.memory.root_pages.StackBucketHeader

; The third argument is the stack's LOW address, and it exists for Windows
; alone (stage 7, brocken 1): Win64 keeps the running stack's bounds in TIB
; fields that the OS itself reads — stack-overflow dispatch, guard-page growth,
; RtlUnwind's frame validation — so a fiber on its own stack has to swap them
; with the rest of the context, and they cannot be derived from the stack TOP.
; Boost's make_fcontext takes a size for the same reason. The SysV and AAPCS64
; files ignore it, which costs them a register they never read; passing it
; unconditionally is what keeps ONE declaration serving every target, and one
; seed with it.
function scaly_make_context(stack_top: pointer[void], fn: pointer[void], stack_base: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void] extern
procedure scaly_jump_context(to: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void] extern

; Evented-I/O backend shim (fiber/eio.c — kqueue/epoll behind one C API).
; Readiness registration is oneshot: an armed fd fires once, then must be
; re-armed. wait blocks until at least one event and fills tags (the void*
; handed to arm). read/write return -2 for would-block, -1 for hard errors.
function scaly_eio_create() returns i32 extern
function scaly_eio_arm(q: i32, fd: i32, for_write: i32, tag: pointer[void]) returns i32 extern
function scaly_eio_wait(q: i32, tags: pointer[void], max: i32) returns i32 extern
; Like scaly_eio_wait but returns 0 if ms elapses before any event (the
; bounded wait behind cross-thread deadlock detection, M2.4 residual).
function scaly_eio_wait_timeout(q: i32, tags: pointer[void], max: i32, ms: i32) returns i32 extern
function scaly_eio_set_nonblocking(fd: i32) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eio_read(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns i64 extern
procedure scaly_eio_write(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns i64 extern

; Cross-thread wake channel (milestone 2.4, shim rule (a): EVFILT_USER is
; kqueue-only, eventfd is linux-only). wake_create registers a persistent
; edge-style user event on q reporting tag when triggered; returns the
; wake handle scaly_eio_wake takes (-1 on failure). scaly_eio_wake may be
; called from ANY thread; triggers before or during a wait complete that
; wait, multiple triggers coalesce into one report.
function scaly_eio_wake_create(q: i32, tag: pointer[void]) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eio_wake(q: i32, w: i32) returns i32 extern

; Close the wake channel (milestone 2.5 residual). Shim-owned (rule a):
; whether the wake handle is a real fd is OS-specific — an eventfd on
; linux (deregistered + closed), a kqueue ident on darwin (the user
; event is deregistered; it dies with the queue anyway).
procedure scaly_eio_wake_close(q: i32, w: i32) returns i32 extern

; Close the poller QUEUE — same argument as the wake close one line up, and
; it was missing for the queue itself: a kqueue/epoll queue is a real fd,
; an IOCP completion port is a HANDLE, and POSIX close(2) on the latter is
; not a leak but a fatal call (the CRT's invalid-parameter handler ends the
; process). Shim-owned by rule (a).
procedure scaly_eio_close(q: i32) returns i32 extern

; Online CPU count (milestone 3.3, shim rule (a): the _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN
; constant's value is OS-specific). The ncpu-based worker default for
; TaskPool.default_pool. Never less than 1.
function scaly_eio_ncpu() returns i32 extern

; Monotonic nanosecond clock (shim rule a: the clockid value is
; OS-specific) — deadline arithmetic for Channel.receive_timeout and
; the Node heartbeat (stage 7, milestone 7.3).
function scaly_eio_now_ns() returns i64 extern

; POSIX close(2) — pool pipes and pollers at thread exit (milestone 2.5
; residual). Declared (int)->(int) to MATCH scalyls's existing declare:
; the whole-program llvm-link sees ONE `close` declaration across the
; linked roots (a type-mismatched pair would conflict).
function close(fd: int) returns i32 extern

; Atomic load (milestone 2.4; cas/store are declared in root_pages.scaly).
; Like all scaly_atomic_* names this is emitter-lowered, never a symbol.
function scaly_atomic_load_u64(p: pointer[u64]) returns u64 extern

; Atomic fetch-add (milestone 3.2 — the task-group pending counter).
; Old-value convention like every scaly_atomic_*; decrement by adding
; (0 - 1) as u64. Emitter-lowered, never a symbol.
function scaly_atomic_add_u64(p: pointer[u64], v: u64) returns u64 extern

; TCP helpers (shim-owned: sockaddr_in layout is OS-specific, and accept
; needs the errno mapping). listen returns a nonblocking listening fd on
; 127.0.0.1:port (0 = ephemeral); accept returns -2 for would-block.
procedure scaly_eio_tcp_listen(port: i32) returns i32 extern
function scaly_eio_tcp_port(fd: i32) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eio_tcp_connect(port: i32) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eio_accept(fd: i32) returns i32 extern

; Stage-7 node runtime (milestone 7.3): INADDR_ANY listener, getaddrinfo
; connect (rule a: addrinfo layout is OS-specific), and the SIGPIPE-safe
; socket write (rule a: MSG_NOSIGNAL/SO_NOSIGPIPE are OS-specific).
procedure scaly_eio_tcp_listen_any(port: i32) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eio_tcp_connect_host(host: pointer[const_char], port: i32) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eio_tcp_write(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns i64 extern

; Guard-page overflow diagnostics (shim-owned: struct sigaction and
; stack_t layouts are OS-specific). Installs a SIGSEGV/SIGBUS handler on
; an alternate stack; classify (a unary Scaly function, signal context —
; reads globals only) decides whether the fault address is a fiber guard
; hit: 1 = report + exit 108, 0 = default action (plain crash).
procedure scaly_guard_install(classify: pointer[void]) returns i32 extern

; Establish the guard region at the low end of a fiber stack (shim rule (a),
; and the sharper form of it: the call exports the QUESTION because the two
; platforms answer it with different MECHANISMS — PROT_NONE on POSIX,
; PAGE_GUARD on Windows). Returns 0 on success.
procedure scaly_stack_guard(base: pointer[void], len: size_t) returns i32 extern

; Thread primitive (milestone 2.1). Direct externs per the containment
; rule: fixed prototypes, POSIX-uniform, no errno. pthread_t is opaque
; and pointer-sized on all four LP64 targets — carried as size_t.
procedure pthread_create(thread: pointer[size_t], attr: pointer[void], start: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void]) returns i32 extern
procedure pthread_join(thread: size_t, retval: pointer[void]) returns i32 extern

; Indirect-call primitive (milestone 2.3b): an extern name with no
; symbol anywhere — the Emitter lowers it to a bare ptr(ptr) indirect
; call, the C calling shape of a pthread-style entry (the delegate
; mechanism can't produce this: it prepends the record as a hidden
; first argument).
function scaly_call_ptr(fn: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void] extern

function scaly_aligned_alloc(alignment: i64, size: i64) returns pointer[void] extern

; ---- Cross-thread deadlock detection (milestone 2.4 residual) ----
;
; A single-thread channel deadlock is caught by exit 106 (Scheduler.run /
; join see the ready queue drained with tasks still blocked). The cross-
; thread case used to hang forever: a receiver parked on a channel whose
; only sender is itself blocked has no local signal that no wake will ever
; come. This closes the SOUND part of that gap without risking a false
; trap.
;
; State (one process-global record, reached by every thread): `threads`
; over-approximates the live threads that could still send or wake (every
; OS thread is counted from spawn to exit — over-counting only ever misses
; a deadlock, never invents one); `blocked` counts threads currently
; inside a channel-motivated poller wait; `epoch` is bumped on every event
; that could wake such a waiter (a queued send, a close, a routed task).
; A channel wait is BOUNDED (CHAN_DEADLOCK_MS): on timeout, if no channel
; event occurred while we waited (epoch unchanged) AND every live thread
; is blocked on a channel (`blocked >= threads`), no send/wake can ever
; arrive, so it is a genuine deadlock → exit 106. Sound because a thread
; not blocked here (running, sleep-polling a bounded send, or waiting on
; real I/O with io_waiting > 0) keeps `blocked < threads`, and any wake in
; flight bumps the epoch or returns the wait with events. It is NOT
; complete: an unrelated idle background thread (e.g. a pooled worker
; parked on its pipe) is counted in `threads` but never in `blocked`, so
; it masks detection — that case still hangs, exactly as before.
define DeadlockState
(
    threads: u64
    blocked: u64
    epoch: u64
)

; Bounded channel-wait timeout: long enough that the idle re-check cost is
; nil (a real wake pokes the poller and returns at once; the timeout only
; bounds detection latency), short enough that a deadlock traps promptly.
define CHAN_DEADLOCK_MS i32 100

; Process-global — NOT thread_local (`shared` declaration, like the heap
; tracer). Allocated once, single-threaded, on first touch (the
; first Thread.spawn or the first channel wait, both on the main thread
; before any concurrency); `threads` starts at 1 for the main thread.
shared deadlock_state: pointer[DeadlockState] null

procedure ensure_deadlock_state()
{
    if deadlock_state <> null
        return
    let d scaly_aligned_alloc(8, (sizeof DeadlockState) as i64) as pointer[DeadlockState]
    set d.threads: 1
    set d.blocked: 0
    set d.epoch: 0
    set deadlock_state: d
}

; Note a channel wake event (queued send / close / routed task) so a
; waiter that observed quiescence just before it does not misread it as a
; deadlock. Cheap no-op until the state exists (no waiter can exist yet).
procedure deadlock_event()
{
    let d deadlock_state
    if d = null
        return
    let ep &d.epoch
    scaly_atomic_add_u64(ep, 1 as u64)
}

; The pthread entry of every Thread.spawn (milestone 2.3b): unpacks the
; spawn cell ({fn, arg} on their own heap page, released BEFORE the
; entry runs — cross-thread frees are safe since 2.3), runs the user
; entry through scaly_call_ptr, then reclaims the dying thread's
; allocator state: pooled fiber stacks, the fully-unwound stack-bucket
; chain, and the heap buckets (empty → freed now; still-live → marked
; dead, freed by their last release_page from any thread). Outside the
; user entry this frame must stay allocation-free — after the reclaim
; there is no allocator left to allocate from.
procedure scaly_thread_start(raw: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]
{
    let cell raw as pointer[size_t]
    let fn_v *cell
    let arg_slot (raw as size_t + 8) as pointer[size_t]
    let arg_v *arg_slot
    let host Page.get(raw)
    Page.release_page_traced(host, "Thread.spawn_cell")
    scaly_call_ptr(fn_v as pointer[void], arg_v as pointer[void])
    ; Milestone-2.5/3.3 residuals: shut down the thread's default task
    ; pool and I/O worker pool (joining workers — each reclaims its own
    ; state through this very trampoline) and close its scheduler's
    ; poller + wake handle, BEFORE the allocator reclaim below (the
    ; drains free pages, and after the reclaim there is no allocator
    ; state left).
    TaskPool.drain_default()
    Io.drain_pool()
    Io.close_poller()
    ; This thread can no longer send or wake — drop it from the deadlock
    ; census so the remaining threads' next bounded wait can conclude.
    let d deadlock_state
    if d <> null
    {
        let tp &d.threads
        scaly_atomic_add_u64(tp, (0 as u64) - (1 as u64))
    }
    Fiber.drain_stack_pool()
    scaly_thread_release_buckets()
    null
}

; POSIX poll(2) — the no-scheduler readiness wait (milestone 2.5).
; struct pollfd (int fd, short events, short revents) and POLLIN=1 /
; POLLOUT=4 are identical on darwin and linux, so per the containment
; rule this is a direct extern, not a shim call. nfds_t is 32-bit on
; darwin and 64-bit on linux: the constant 1 passed as i32 materializes
; with the upper register half zeroed on both ABIs.
; nfds is `nfds_t`: 32 bits on macOS, 64 on glibc. `u64` is portable both ways
; (the caller writes the whole register; a 32-bit callee reads the low half),
; and it keeps this declaration type-identical to the one in scalyls/worker.scaly
; -- both land in the same linked binary.
procedure poll(fds: pointer[void], nfds: u64, timeout: i32) returns i32 extern

; POSIX pipe(2) — the worker pool's blocking wait channel (milestone
; 2.5). int[2] on both OSes: direct extern.
procedure pipe(fds: pointer[i32]) returns i32 extern

; Worker-pool cap per requesting thread (milestone 2.5). Beyond it a
; queued item waits for the next worker to come free.
define IO_POOL_WORKERS i64 4

; Stack provisioning. 32-bit C params must be i32, not int (Apple arm64
; stack-arg ABI); mmap's offset is 64-bit on both OSes.
function mmap(addr: pointer[void], length: size_t, prot: i32, flags: i32, fd: i32, offset: i64) returns pointer[void] extern
function munmap(addr: pointer[void], length: size_t) returns i32 extern
function mprotect(addr: pointer[void], len: size_t, prot: i32) returns i32 extern
function getpagesize() returns i32 extern

; MAP_PRIVATE (0x2, both OSes) | MAP_ANON (darwin, 0x1000) | MAP_ANONYMOUS
; (linux, 0x20). The union works on both OSes: 0x1000 on linux is
; MAP_EXECUTABLE and 0x20 on darwin is MAP_RENAME — both documented as
; ignored. Verified on arm64-darwin; the linux legs run in CI.
define MAP_ANON_PRIVATE i32 0x1022
define PROT_NONE i32 0
define PROT_READ_WRITE i32 3

; Default fiber stack: 128 KB including the guard page (no growing in v1).
define FIBER_STACK_SIZE size_t 0x20000

; The running fiber (null = main context). Single-threaded by design in
; stage 1; becomes per-thread state in stage 2.
mutable current_fiber: pointer[void] null

; The active scheduler (pointer[Scheduler]) and the task it is currently
; running (pointer[SchedTask], null = main context). Single-threaded by
; design in stage 1, like current_fiber.
mutable current_scheduler: pointer[void] null
mutable current_task: pointer[void] null

; Wholesale-teardown counters. fiber_regions_freed ticks ONCE PER DISPOSED
; FIBER and is THE teardown proof (Fiber.freed_region_count) — use it in
; tests. The finer-grained pair underneath needs care: a cleanly returned
; fiber POPS its root pages as its frames unwind, so fiber_pages_freed only
; grows for fibers torn down mid-flight, and fiber_buckets_freed counts
; allocator caches (nonzero only when the fiber allocated at all).
mutable fiber_regions_freed: i64 0
mutable fiber_pages_freed: i64 0
mutable fiber_buckets_freed: i64 0

; The stack pool (milestone 2.0): a LIFO of freed DEFAULT-SIZED fiber
; stacks, threaded through the stacks themselves — the entry is two
; size_t slots [next, size] at stack_base + guard page. Single-threaded
; like every fiber global (per-thread in stage 2). Custom sizes are not
; pooled: a mixed pool would let an odd-sized head block reuse.
mutable stack_pool: pointer[void] null
mutable fiber_stacks_pooled: i64 0
mutable fiber_stacks_reused: i64 0

; This thread's blocking-operation worker pool (milestone 2.5,
; pointer[IoPool]; null until the first Io.offload from a task) and the
; count of operations shipped to a worker — the suspension proof for
; tests. Thread-local like every mutable global: each requesting thread
; grows its own pool.
mutable io_pool: pointer[void] null
mutable io_offloads: i64 0

; This thread's poller/wake fd MIRRORS (milestone 2.5 residual), stored
; as fd+1 so the constant initializer 0 means "none". close_poller at
; thread exit reads THESE, never the Scheduler record — a thread entry
; may have released the scheduler's host page before returning, and a
; freed heap page can be re-allocated by another thread at any moment.
mutable io_poller_fd: int 0
mutable io_wake_fd: int 0

; This thread's default task pool (milestone 3.3, pointer[TaskPool];
; null until the first TaskPool.default_pool call — the promoted-for
; dispatch target). Thread-local like the I/O pool: the pool pointer
; rides a TLS cell, no process-global mutable state needed. A spawned
; thread's pool is shut down by the exit trampoline (drain_default);
; the main thread's lives until process exit.
mutable task_default_pool: pointer[void] null

; Guard-page fault classifier state: installed once, reads only globals
; (it runs in signal context). guard_page_size is cached at create time
; so the classifier calls nothing at all.
mutable guard_installed: bool false
mutable guard_page_size: size_t 0

define Fiber
(
    ctx: pointer[void]           ; the suspended (non-running) side's context
    stack_base: pointer[void]    ; mmap base; guard page at the bottom
    stack_size: size_t
    saved_head: pointer[StackBucketHeader]  ; the non-running side's ...
    saved_top: pointer[Page]                ; ... StackBucket allocator state
    done: bool
)
{
    ; How many fiber regions have been disposed — one tick per fiber,
    ; whether or not it ever allocated. The teardown proof for tests.
    function freed_region_count() returns i64
        fiber_regions_freed

    function freed_page_count() returns i64
        fiber_pages_freed

    function freed_bucket_count() returns i64
        fiber_buckets_freed

    function pooled_stack_count() returns i64
        fiber_stacks_pooled

    function reused_stack_count() returns i64
        fiber_stacks_reused

    ; Pop a pooled stack (base address) when the pool head matches the
    ; requested size; null otherwise. Only default-sized stacks are ever
    ; pooled, so a custom-size request simply misses.
    procedure take_pooled_stack(size: size_t) returns pointer[void]
    {
        let e stack_pool
        if e = null
            return null
        let entry e as pointer[size_t]
        let esize *(((e as size_t) + 8) as pointer[size_t])
        if esize <> size
            return null
        set stack_pool: (*entry) as pointer[void]
        set fiber_stacks_pooled: fiber_stacks_pooled - 1
        set fiber_stacks_reused: fiber_stacks_reused + 1
        ((e as size_t) - guard_page_size) as pointer[void]
    }

    ; Hand every pooled stack back to the OS.
    procedure drain_stack_pool()
    {
        var e stack_pool
        while e <> null
        {
            let entry e as pointer[size_t]
            let next (*entry) as pointer[void]
            let size *(((e as size_t) + 8) as pointer[size_t])
            let base ((e as size_t) - guard_page_size) as pointer[void]
            let mrc munmap(base, size)
            if mrc <> 0
                exit 105
            set fiber_stacks_pooled: fiber_stacks_pooled - 1
            set e: next
        }
        set stack_pool: null
    }

    ; Swap the StackBucket allocator state between the process globals and
    ; the record. Called at every context crossing, keeping the invariant
    ; that the record holds the NON-running side's state.
    procedure swap_allocator(f: ref[Fiber])
    {
        let h stack_head
        let t stack_top
        set stack_head: f.saved_head
        set stack_top: f.saved_top
        set f.saved_head: h
        set f.saved_top: t
    }

    ; Create a suspended fiber. The record lives on the given page; the
    ; entry function fn (take it with &my_fiber) runs on a pooled or
    ; freshly mmap'd stack with a guard page at the low end,
    ; and allocates into its own region. The first create installs the
    ; guard-page overflow handler (best-effort diagnostic — a failed
    ; install just keeps the plain-crash behavior).
    procedure create_sized(host: ref[Page], fn: pointer[void], size: size_t) returns ref[Fiber]
    {
        let f host.allocate(sizeof Fiber, alignof Fiber) as pointer[Fiber]
        let guard_size getpagesize() as size_t
        set guard_page_size: guard_size
        if not guard_installed
        {
            set guard_installed: true
            scaly_guard_install(&fiber_guard_hit)
        }
        var base Fiber.take_pooled_stack(size)
        if base = null
        {
            set base: mmap(null, size, PROT_READ_WRITE, MAP_ANON_PRIVATE, (0 - 1) as i32, 0 as i64)
            if (base as i64) = ((0 - 1) as i64)
                exit 103
            ; Through the shim, not mprotect directly: "make this the stack's
            ; overflow guard" is the QUESTION, and the two platforms answer it
            ; with different mechanisms — PROT_NONE on POSIX, PAGE_GUARD on
            ; Windows, where an inaccessible page would leave the kernel no
            ; room to build the exception record and the overflow would die
            ; before the handler could name it.
            let grc scaly_stack_guard(base, guard_size)
            if grc <> 0
                exit 104
        }
        let top ((base as size_t) + size) as pointer[void]
        set f.ctx: scaly_make_context(top, fn, base)
        set f.stack_base: base
        set f.stack_size: size
        set f.saved_head: null    ; fresh region: the fiber's first local page
        set f.saved_top: null     ; creates its own bucket chain
        set f.done: false
        f
    }

    procedure create(host: ref[Page], fn: pointer[void]) returns ref[Fiber]
    {
        let size FIBER_STACK_SIZE
        Fiber.create_sized(host, fn, size)
    }

    ; Run the fiber until it yields or finishes. Returns true when the
    ; fiber can be resumed again, false once it finished (or was done).
    procedure resume(f: ref[Fiber]) returns bool
    {
        if f.done
            return false
        let prev current_fiber
        set current_fiber: f as pointer[void]
        Fiber.swap_allocator(f)
        set f.ctx: scaly_jump_context(f.ctx)
        set current_fiber: prev
        not f.done
    }

    ; First statement of every fiber entry function: stores the resumer's
    ; context (the entry argument) into the record, establishing the
    ; record invariant. The resume that jumped here already swapped the
    ; allocator state.
    procedure started(first_ctx: pointer[void])
    {
        let f current_fiber as ref[Fiber]
        set f.ctx: first_ctx
    }

    ; Suspend the running fiber and return to its resumer. Returns when
    ; the fiber is resumed again.
    procedure yield()
    {
        let f current_fiber as ref[Fiber]
        Fiber.swap_allocator(f)
        set f.ctx: scaly_jump_context(f.ctx)
    }

    ; Finish the running fiber: marks it done and returns to the resumer
    ; for good. Never returns (resume refuses a done fiber; if the entry
    ; function itself ever returned, the switcher traps).
    procedure finish()
    {
        let f current_fiber as ref[Fiber]
        set f.done: true
        Fiber.swap_allocator(f)
        scaly_jump_context(f.ctx)
    }

    ; Free the fiber's whole region and stack: deallocate the extension
    ; and exclusive chains of every root page still on the fiber's stack
    ; (they return to the shared heap buckets), free the fiber's stack
    ; buckets, then munmap the fiber stack. Returns the number of root
    ; pages torn down. Call after the fiber finished (or to kill it — a
    ; disposed fiber must never be resumed).
    procedure dispose(f: ref[Fiber]) returns int
    {
        var pages_freed 0
        var bucket f.saved_head
        ; Buckets from the head up to saved_top's bucket hold live root
        ; pages (in the top bucket only up to saved_top); buckets beyond
        ; that are empty caches kept by allocate_root_page — freed below,
        ; nothing to tear down inside.
        var live true
        if f.saved_top = null
            set live: false
        while bucket <> null
        {
            if live
            {
                var p first_usable_page(bucket as pointer[void])
                var last last_usable_page(bucket as pointer[void])
                let top_bucket stack_bucket_of(f.saved_top)
                if bucket = top_bucket
                {
                    set last: f.saved_top
                    set live: false
                }
                while (p as size_t) <= (last as size_t)
                {
                    p.deallocate_extensions()
                    set pages_freed: pages_freed + 1
                    set p: ((p as size_t) + PAGE_SIZE) as pointer[Page]
                }
            }
            let next_bucket bucket.next
            scaly_aligned_free(bucket as pointer[void])
            set fiber_buckets_freed: fiber_buckets_freed + 1
            set bucket: next_bucket
        }
        set f.saved_head: null
        set f.saved_top: null
        ; Default-sized stacks go to the pool (entry = [next, size] right
        ; above the guard page); custom sizes go back to the OS.
        if f.stack_size = FIBER_STACK_SIZE
        {
            let e ((f.stack_base as size_t) + guard_page_size) as pointer[size_t]
            set *e: stack_pool as size_t
            set *(((e as size_t) + 8) as pointer[size_t]): f.stack_size
            set stack_pool: e as pointer[void]
            set fiber_stacks_pooled: fiber_stacks_pooled + 1
        }
        else
        {
            let mrc munmap(f.stack_base, f.stack_size)
            if mrc <> 0
                exit 105
        }
        set fiber_pages_freed: fiber_pages_freed + (pages_freed as i64)
        set fiber_regions_freed: fiber_regions_freed + 1
        pages_freed
    }
}

; Is addr inside the RUNNING fiber's guard page? The overflow classifier
; the shim's signal handler calls (signal context: reads globals only,
; calls nothing). Only running code can overflow its own stack, so the
; current fiber suffices; on the main context (or a miss) the handler
; falls through to the default action.
function fiber_guard_hit(addr: pointer[void]) returns i32
{
    let f current_fiber as ref[Fiber]?
    if f = null
        return 0 as i32
    let lo f.stack_base as size_t
    let hi lo + guard_page_size
    let a addr as size_t
    if a >= lo
    {
        if a < hi
            return 1 as i32
    }
    0 as i32
}

; A scheduled task. The entry function pointer MUST stay the FIRST field:
; the record doubles as the delegate closure record scheduler_entry calls
; through (slot 0 = thunk). The next link serves double duty — ready-queue
; link while the task is queued, waiter-chain link while it is blocked in
; join (a task is never both).
define SchedTask
(
    entry: pointer[void]         ; the task's zero-argument procedure
    fiber: ref[Fiber]
    next: ref[SchedTask]?
    waiter: ref[SchedTask]?   ; tasks blocked joining this one
    done: bool
    disposed: bool
    arg: pointer[void]           ; spawn_arg's argument (task_arg reads it)
    ; home: the spawning scheduler (milestone 2.4) — a remote channel
    ; sender routes wakes through it.
    home: pointer[void]
)

; The calling shape of a task record viewed as a delegate value: the
; thunk in slot 0 receives the record pointer itself as a hidden first
; argument, which a zero-argument Scaly procedure ignores (safe on both
; LP64 ABIs — the argument register is simply unread).
define TaskFn delegate() returns void

; The fiber entry of every scheduled task: wraps the started/finish
; protocol around an indirect call of the task's own procedure. The
; dispatcher stores the task into current_task before resuming.
procedure scheduler_entry(first_ctx: pointer[void])
{
    Fiber.started first_ctx
    let f current_task as TaskFn
    f()
    Fiber.finish()
}

define Scheduler
(
    host: pointer[Page]          ; task records, fibers, and queue live here
    head: ref[SchedTask]?     ; FIFO ready queue
    tail: ref[SchedTask]?
    blocked: int                 ; tasks waiting in a join, send/receive, or Io
    eio: i32                     ; readiness poller fd (-1 until first Io use)
    io_waiting: int              ; tasks parked on fd readiness
    wake: i32                    ; cross-thread wake handle (-1 until armed)
    inject: u64                  ; MPSC inject queue: Treiber stack of
                                 ; SchedTasks pushed by remote threads
    chan_parked: int             ; tasks parked on a Channel receive
)
{
    ; Create the scheduler on the given page and make it current. All
    ; spawned task records and fiber stacks are anchored on this page,
    ; so they survive any spawning fiber's region teardown.
    procedure create(host: ref[Page]) returns ref[Scheduler]
    {
        let s host.allocate(sizeof Scheduler, alignof Scheduler) as pointer[Scheduler]
        set s.host: host
        set s.head: null
        set s.tail: null
        set s.blocked: 0
        set s.eio: (0 - 1) as i32
        set s.io_waiting: 0
        set s.wake: (0 - 1) as i32
        set s.inject: 0
        set s.chan_parked: 0
        set current_scheduler: s as pointer[void]
        s
    }

    ; Append a task to the ready-queue tail (round-robin order).
    procedure enqueue(t: ref[SchedTask])
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        set t.next: null
        if s.tail = null
        {
            set s.head: t
            set s.tail: t
            return
        }
        let tl s.tail
        set tl.next: t
        set s.tail: t
    }

    ; Pop the ready-queue head (null when the queue is empty).
    procedure dequeue() returns ref[SchedTask]?
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        let t s.head
        if t <> null
        {
            set s.head: t.next
            if s.head = null
                set s.tail: null
            set t.next: null
        }
        t
    }

    ; Create a task for the given zero-argument procedure (take it with
    ; &my_task) on a fiber stack of the given size, hand it one
    ; pointer-sized argument (readable inside the task via
    ; Scheduler.task_arg — cast an fd through size_t), and put it at the
    ; ready-queue tail. Callable from the main context and from inside a
    ; running task alike. Non-default stack sizes bypass the stack pool.
    procedure spawn_sized(fn: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void], size: size_t) returns ref[SchedTask]
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        let host s.host
        let t host.allocate(sizeof SchedTask, alignof SchedTask) as pointer[SchedTask]
        set t.entry: fn
        set t.fiber: Fiber.create_sized(host, &scheduler_entry, size)
        set t.next: null
        set t.waiter: null
        set t.done: false
        set t.disposed: false
        set t.arg: arg
        set t.home: s as pointer[void]
        Scheduler.enqueue(t)
        t
    }

    ; spawn_sized with the default stack size.
    procedure spawn_arg(fn: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void]) returns ref[SchedTask]
    {
        let size FIBER_STACK_SIZE
        Scheduler.spawn_sized(fn, arg, size)
    }

    ; spawn_arg without an argument.
    procedure spawn(fn: pointer[void]) returns ref[SchedTask]
        Scheduler.spawn_arg(fn, null)

    ; The running task's spawn_arg argument (null on the main context).
    procedure task_arg() returns pointer[void]
    {
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        if me = null
            return null
        me.arg
    }

    ; Give up the current time slice: requeue the running task at the
    ; tail and return to the dispatcher. A no-op on the main context.
    procedure yield()
    {
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        if me <> null
        {
            Scheduler.enqueue(me)
            Fiber.yield()
        }
    }

    ; Push a channel receiver onto its HOME scheduler's inject queue and
    ; poke that scheduler's wake handle (milestone 2.4). Called from a
    ; REMOTE thread: the only shared state touched is the atomic Treiber
    ; head (the task's next field is ours to write until the CAS
    ; publishes it) and the wake syscall; the home scheduler's counters
    ; are adjusted by its own drain_inject. The receiver armed the wake
    ; handle before parking, so the racy read of home.eio/home.wake sees
    ; initialized values (published by the channel lock).
    procedure inject_task(home: pointer[Scheduler], t: ref[SchedTask])
    {
        let hp &home.inject
        repeat
        {
            let h scaly_atomic_load_u64(hp)
            set t.next: (h as size_t) as pointer[SchedTask]
            if scaly_atomic_cas_u64(hp, h, t as u64) = h
                break
        }
        Scheduler.wake_remote(home)
    }

    ; Poke a scheduler's wake handle so a poll_io blocked on it returns.
    ; Safe from any thread; a scheduler that never armed a wake (no
    ; channel receive yet) is simply not waiting — nothing to poke.
    procedure wake_remote(home: ref[Scheduler])
    {
        let q home.eio
        if q = ((0 - 1) as i32)
            return
        let w home.wake
        if w = ((0 - 1) as i32)
            return
        scaly_eio_wake(q, w)
    }

    ; Move every remotely-injected channel receiver to the ready queue
    ; (milestone 2.4). Runs only on the scheduler's own thread (start of
    ; every step), so the counter updates are owner-writes; the detach
    ; CAS is the only contended access. The Treiber stack is LIFO —
    ; reversed here so cross-thread wakes keep arrival order.
    procedure drain_inject()
    {
        let s current_scheduler as pointer[Scheduler]
        let hp &s.inject
        var h scaly_atomic_load_u64(hp)
        if h = 0
            return
        repeat
        {
            let got scaly_atomic_cas_u64(hp, h, 0 as u64)
            if got = h
                break
            set h: got
        }
        var rev: ref[SchedTask]? null
        var t (h as size_t) as pointer[SchedTask]
        while t <> null
        {
            let nx t.next
            set t.next: rev
            set rev: t
            set t: nx
        }
        while rev <> null
        {
            let nx rev.next
            Scheduler.enqueue(rev)
            set s.blocked: s.blocked - 1
            set s.chan_parked: s.chan_parked - 1
            set rev: nx
        }
    }

    ; Run one ready task until it yields or finishes. A task that
    ; finished is reaped ON THE SPOT — its whole fiber region (stack,
    ; buckets, pages) dies with it, milestone 1.6's "the region dies
    ; with the connection" — after waking its joiners. Returns false
    ; when the ready queue is empty. Remotely-injected channel
    ; receivers are drained into the ready queue first (every dispatch
    ; loop runs through here, so no loop needs its own drain).
    procedure step() returns bool
    {
        Scheduler.drain_inject()
        let t Scheduler.dequeue()
        if t = null
            return false
        set current_task: t as pointer[void]
        let more Fiber.resume(t.fiber)
        set current_task: null
        if not more
        {
            set t.done: true
            Scheduler.wake_waiters(t)
            Scheduler.reap(t)
        }
        true
    }

    ; Move every task blocked on t back to the ready queue.
    procedure wake_waiters(t: ref[SchedTask])
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        var w t.waiter
        while w <> null
        {
            let n w.next
            Scheduler.enqueue(w)
            set s.blocked: s.blocked - 1
            set w: n
        }
        set t.waiter: null
    }

    ; Block in the poller until at least one armed fd fires, then wake
    ; every parked task whose readiness arrived (requeue, unblock).
    ; Returns true when the main context's own event (null tag) fired.
    ; Only ever runs on the main context (the dispatcher).
    procedure poll_io() returns bool
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        var tagbuf u8[512]
        var n: i32 0
        if s.io_waiting > 0
        {
            ; At least one fd-readiness waiter: block until the OS reports
            ; progress. External I/O is never a channel deadlock, so this
            ; leg is unbounded exactly as before.
            set n: scaly_eio_wait(s.eio, tagbuf as pointer[void], 64 as i32)
        }
        else
        {
            ; Purely channel-motivated (task channel parks, or a main-
            ; context channel wait): bound the wait so a global channel
            ; deadlock is trapped (exit 106) instead of hanging. Mark this
            ; thread blocked around the wait; on a timeout with no channel
            ; event (epoch stable) and every live thread blocked, no wake
            ; can ever come.
            ensure_deadlock_state()
            let d deadlock_state
            let ep &d.epoch
            let bp &d.blocked
            let tp &d.threads
            let e0 scaly_atomic_load_u64(ep)
            scaly_atomic_add_u64(bp, 1 as u64)
            set n: scaly_eio_wait_timeout(s.eio, tagbuf as pointer[void], 64 as i32, CHAN_DEADLOCK_MS)
            if n = 0
            {
                let e1 scaly_atomic_load_u64(ep)
                if e1 = e0
                {
                    let nb scaly_atomic_load_u64(bp)
                    let nt scaly_atomic_load_u64(tp)
                    if nb >= nt
                        exit 106
                }
            }
            scaly_atomic_add_u64(bp, (0 as u64) - (1 as u64))
        }
        if n = ((0 - 1) as i32)
            exit 107
        var main_woke false
        var i 0
        while i < (n as i64)
        {
            let raw *(((tagbuf as size_t) + ((i * 8) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t])
            if raw = 0
            {
                set main_woke: true
            }
            else
            {
                if raw = 1
                {
                    ; The wake handle fired (milestone 2.4): a remote
                    ; thread pushed onto the inject queue or closed a
                    ; channel. Nothing to do here — the next step()
                    ; drains the inject queue, and a main-context
                    ; channel waiter re-checks its queue on return.
                }
                else
                {
                    let t raw as pointer[SchedTask]
                    Scheduler.enqueue(t)
                    set s.blocked: s.blocked - 1
                    set s.io_waiting: s.io_waiting - 1
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        main_woke
    }

    ; Run one ready task; when the ready queue is empty but tasks await
    ; fd readiness, block in the poller and wake them. Tasks parked on a
    ; Channel receive (milestone 2.4) also justify blocking: a remote
    ; sender's inject + wake (or the last sender's close) gets them
    ; moving again — that is the cross-thread replacement for exit 106,
    ; which keeps its exact meaning for programs without channel parks.
    ; Returns false only when nothing can run again without outside
    ; help — the caller decides whether remaining blocked tasks make
    ; that a deadlock.
    procedure step_or_wait() returns bool
    {
        if Scheduler.step()
            return true
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        if s.io_waiting > 0
        {
            Scheduler.poll_io()
            return true
        }
        if s.chan_parked > 0
        {
            Scheduler.poll_io()
            return true
        }
        false
    }

    ; Dispose a finished task's fiber region exactly once. The
    ; dispatcher already reaps eagerly (step), so this normally finds
    ; the task disposed and returns 0; it stays as the idempotence
    ; anchor for join and for callers holding a task record. (A cleanly
    ; returned task leaves 0 live pages anyway — its frames unwound
    ; before finish; watch Fiber.freed_bucket_count for the teardown
    ; proof.)
    procedure reap(t: ref[SchedTask]) returns int
    {
        if t.disposed
            return 0
        set t.disposed: true
        Fiber.dispose(t.fiber)
    }

    ; Round-robin over the ready queue until it drains, blocking in the
    ; poller while I/O waiters remain. Traps (exit 106) if tasks are
    ; still blocked then — a join/channel deadlock.
    procedure run()
    {
        while Scheduler.step_or_wait()
        {
        }
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        if s.blocked > 0
            exit 106
    }

    ; Wait until t finished. The dispatcher reaps eagerly, so the
    ; fiber's region is normally gone already (the reap here returns 0
    ; then — it only frees when a caller disposed nothing yet). From
    ; the main context this drives the dispatcher itself; from inside a
    ; task it blocks on t's waiter chain and yields to the dispatcher.
    procedure join(t: ref[SchedTask]) returns int
    {
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        if me = null
        {
            while not t.done
            {
                if not Scheduler.step_or_wait()
                    exit 106
            }
            return Scheduler.reap(t)
        }
        if not t.done
        {
            let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
            set me.next: t.waiter
            set t.waiter: me
            set s.blocked: s.blocked + 1
            Fiber.yield()
        }
        Scheduler.reap(t)
    }
}

; One queued blocking operation (milestone 2.5). The record lives on
; its own heap page (requester-allocated, requester-freed after the
; result is consumed); the worker writes result before the inject that
; wakes the requester publishes it.
define IoWork
(
    next: ref[IoWork]?
    fn: pointer[void]            ; procedure fn(arg) returns pointer[void]
    arg: pointer[void]
    result: pointer[void]
    task: ref[SchedTask]     ; the parked requester
)

; A requesting thread's worker pool (milestone 2.5): a spinlocked work
; FIFO plus a pipe whose bytes count queued items — an idle worker
; blocks in a 1-byte read, so waiting needs no condvars (their struct
; layouts are OS-specific and would grow the shim). The record lives on
; its own heap page: workers reach it through their spawn argument,
; never through the requester's thread-local globals.
define IoPool
(
    lock: u64                    ; spinlock (bucket_lock pattern)
    head: ref[IoWork]?        ; queued operations, FIFO
    tail: ref[IoWork]?
    pipe_read: i32               ; workers block reading here
    pipe_write: i32              ; one byte written per queued item
    workers: i64                 ; spawned workers (grows to the cap
                                 ; until drain_pool resets the pool)
    idle: i64                    ; workers blocked in the pipe read
    ; Worker join handles (IO_POOL_WORKERS slots on the pool page) —
    ; drain_pool joins each. Requester-written only (one requesting
    ; thread per pool), so no lock is needed.
    handles: pointer[size_t]
)
{
    ; The pool spinlock — same CAS pattern as the channel and the
    ; root_pages bucket_lock: critical sections are a few pointer
    ; moves, one lock at a time.
    procedure acquire(pl: pointer[IoPool])
    {
        let lp &pl.lock
        repeat
        {
            if scaly_atomic_cas_u64(lp, 0 as u64, 1 as u64) = (0 as u64)
                break
        }
    }

    procedure release(pl: pointer[IoPool])
    {
        let lp &pl.lock
        scaly_atomic_store_u64(lp, 0 as u64)
    }
}

; A pool worker (milestone 2.5): block in the 1-byte pipe read (one
; byte = one queued item), pop one operation, run it, publish the
; result, wake the requesting task through its home scheduler's inject
; queue — exactly a remote channel sender's wake path. Runs on its own
; OS thread: everything it touches arrives through the pool pointer
; (the requester's thread-local globals are invisible here — the
; milestone-2.4 lesson). Allocation happens only inside the shipped
; operation, on this worker's own thread-local allocator; a result
; lives on its own heap page, freed cross-thread by the requester
; (safe since milestone 2.3). The loop never returns.
procedure io_worker(arg: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]
{
    let pl arg as ref[IoPool]
    repeat
    {
        IoPool.acquire(pl)
        set pl.idle: pl.idle + 1
        IoPool.release(pl)
        var byte u8[1]
        let n scaly_eio_read(pl.pipe_read, byte as pointer[void], 1 as size_t)
        if n <> 1
            return null
        IoPool.acquire(pl)
        set pl.idle: pl.idle - 1
        let w pl.head
        if w <> null
        {
            set pl.head: w.next
            if pl.head = null
                set pl.tail: null
            set w.next: null
        }
        IoPool.release(pl)
        if w <> null
        {
            set w.result: scaly_call_ptr(w.fn, w.arg)
            let t w.task
            Scheduler.inject_task(t.home as pointer[Scheduler], t)
        }
    }
}

; Suspending I/O over the scheduler's readiness poller (milestone 1.5).
; A file descriptor must be nonblocking (Io.set_nonblocking, once); read
; and write try the syscall and park the running task on would-block.
; The backend (kqueue/epoll) lives in the eio C shim — see the module
; header. At most one parked task per fd at a time. Since milestone 2.5
; no scheduler is required: without one, would-block waits in poll(2).
define Io
{
    ; The current scheduler's poller fd, created lazily on first use.
    ; (i32-to-i64 casts zero-extend, so a sign test must compare against
    ; -1 as i32 — the shim's failure value — not cast and check < 0.)
    procedure ensure_poll() returns i32
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        var q s.eio
        if q = ((0 - 1) as i32)
        {
            ; This scheduler has no poller yet, so a non-zero fd mirror
            ; belongs to a PRIOR scheduler on this thread (the sequential-
            ; schedulers-on-one-thread pattern — M2.4/2.5 residual). Reap
            ; it now instead of leaking its poller + wake fds; the prior
            ; scheduler is defunct (this thread moved on to a new one).
            Io.close_poller()
            set q: scaly_eio_create()
            if q = ((0 - 1) as i32)
                exit 107
            set s.eio: q
            set io_poller_fd: (q as int) + 1
        }
        q
    }

    ; Arm the current scheduler's cross-thread wake handle (milestone
    ; 2.4), creating the poller first if needed. Idempotent; called by
    ; Channel.receive BEFORE any park so a remote sender's wake_remote
    ; always finds initialized eio/wake fields. The tag is the sentinel
    ; 1 — poll_io recognizes it as "drain the inject queue / re-check
    ; the channel" (0 stays the main context's own-event tag, real
    ; pointers stay Io-parked tasks).
    procedure ensure_wake() returns i32
    {
        let q Io.ensure_poll()
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        var w s.wake
        if w = ((0 - 1) as i32)
        {
            let tag (1 as size_t) as pointer[void]
            set w: scaly_eio_wake_create(q, tag)
            if w = ((0 - 1) as i32)
                exit 107
            set s.wake: w
            set io_wake_fd: (w as int) + 1
        }
        w
    }

    ; Put the fd into nonblocking mode (fcntl lives in the shim: it is
    ; variadic in libc, unreachable through a fixed-prototype extern).
    procedure set_nonblocking(fd: i32)
    {
        let rc scaly_eio_set_nonblocking(fd)
        if rc <> 0
            exit 107
    }

    ; Block until fd is ready, scheduler-free (milestone 2.5): a plain
    ; poll(2) on one fd, infinite timeout. The result is advisory — the
    ; caller retries its syscall, which reports any hard error itself
    ; (an EINTR or POLLNVAL just loops through one more retry).
    procedure wait_ready(fd: i32, for_write: bool)
    {
        var pfd u8[8]
        set *(pfd as pointer[i32]): fd
        ; POLLIN=1 / POLLOUT=4 on both OSes. Inlined, not module
        ; constants: scalyls defines its own POLLIN and the two global
        ; symbols would collide in a whole-stack link.
        var ev: i32 1
        if for_write
            set ev: 4
        ; events is the u16 at offset 4 (revents at 6 is poll's to fill).
        set *(((pfd as size_t) + 4) as pointer[u16]): ev as u16
        poll(pfd as pointer[void], 1 as u64, (0 - 1) as i32)
    }

    ; Park until fd is ready. A task arms the fd (oneshot, tagged with
    ; its record) and yields to the dispatcher; the main context arms
    ; with a null tag and drives the dispatcher until its event fires,
    ; running ready tasks first (mirrors a main-context join). Without
    ; any scheduler (milestone 2.5) the wait is a plain poll(2).
    procedure park(fd: i32, for_write: bool)
    {
        if current_scheduler = null
        {
            Io.wait_ready(fd, for_write)
            return
        }
        let q Io.ensure_poll()
        var wr: i32 0
        if for_write
            set wr: 1
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        if me = null
        {
            let arc scaly_eio_arm(q, fd, wr, null)
            if arc <> 0
                exit 107
            ; The armed fd makes this an EXTERNAL I/O wait: count it, so
            ; poll_io blocks unbounded instead of taking the channel-
            ; deadlock-bounded branch (a main-context accept/read under
            ; a scheduler exited 106 spuriously otherwise — found by the
            ; stage-7 node tests). The null tag is the main context's
            ; own event; poll_io only decrements TASK waiters, so the
            ; count is undone here.
            let sm current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
            set sm.io_waiting: sm.io_waiting + 1
            repeat
            {
                if not Scheduler.step()
                {
                    if Scheduler.poll_io()
                    {
                        set sm.io_waiting: sm.io_waiting - 1
                        return
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        let arc2 scaly_eio_arm(q, fd, wr, me as pointer[void])
        if arc2 <> 0
            exit 107
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        set s.blocked: s.blocked + 1
        set s.io_waiting: s.io_waiting + 1
        Fiber.yield()
    }

    ; Read up to count bytes; suspends while the fd has nothing to
    ; offer. Returns the byte count, 0 at EOF, -1 on a hard error.
    procedure read(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns i64
    {
        var r scaly_eio_read(fd, buf, count)
        while r = ((0 - 2) as i64)
        {
            Io.park(fd, false)
            set r: scaly_eio_read(fd, buf, count)
        }
        r
    }

    ; Write up to count bytes; suspends while the fd cannot take data.
    ; Returns the byte count written (partial writes possible), -1 on a
    ; hard error.
    procedure write(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns i64
    {
        var r scaly_eio_write(fd, buf, count)
        while r = ((0 - 2) as i64)
        {
            Io.park(fd, true)
            set r: scaly_eio_write(fd, buf, count)
        }
        r
    }

    ; A nonblocking TCP socket listening on 127.0.0.1:port (0 asks the
    ; kernel for an ephemeral port; Io.local_port reads the assignment).
    ; Returns the fd, -1 on failure. Socket setup lives in the shim:
    ; sockaddr_in's layout is OS-specific.
    procedure listen(port: i32) returns i32
        scaly_eio_tcp_listen(port)

    ; The local port a bound socket ended up on (-1 on failure).
    function local_port(fd: i32) returns i32
        scaly_eio_tcp_port(fd)

    ; Connect to 127.0.0.1:port — blocking handshake (instant against a
    ; live local listener); call Io.set_nonblocking on the result before
    ; suspending I/O. Returns the fd, -1 on failure.
    procedure connect(port: i32) returns i32
        scaly_eio_tcp_connect(port)

    ; Accept a connection; suspends while none is pending (readiness on
    ; a listening fd = pending connection). Returns the new fd (still
    ; blocking — Io.set_nonblocking it for suspending I/O), -1 on a
    ; hard error. (Sign tests on i32 must compare against -1 as i32:
    ; casts zero-extend.)
    procedure accept(fd: i32) returns i32
    {
        var r scaly_eio_accept(fd)
        while r = ((0 - 2) as i32)
        {
            Io.park(fd, false)
            set r: scaly_eio_accept(fd)
        }
        r
    }

    ; Listen on ALL interfaces (INADDR_ANY) — a cluster node accepting
    ; real remote peers (stage 7, milestone 7.3). Same contract as
    ; Io.listen otherwise.
    procedure listen_any(port: i32) returns i32
        scaly_eio_tcp_listen_any(port)

    ; Resolve host by NAME (getaddrinfo in the shim, rule a) and
    ; connect — blocking handshake like Io.connect. host must be
    ; NUL-terminated (String.to_c_string). Returns the fd, -1 on
    ; failure (resolution or connection).
    procedure connect_host(host: pointer[const_char], port: i32) returns i32
        scaly_eio_tcp_connect_host(host, port)

    ; SIGPIPE-safe socket write (stage 7): a write racing a peer's death
    ; must return -1/EPIPE, never kill the process. Suspension contract
    ; of Io.write otherwise.
    procedure tcp_write(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns i64
    {
        var r scaly_eio_tcp_write(fd, buf, count)
        while r = ((0 - 2) as i64)
        {
            Io.park(fd, true)
            set r: scaly_eio_tcp_write(fd, buf, count)
        }
        r
    }

    ; Whole-buffer variant of tcp_write (the Node frame writer). False
    ; on a hard error — the caller treats that as peer suspicion, not a
    ; program error.
    procedure tcp_write_all(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns bool
    {
        var done: size_t 0
        while done < count
        {
            let r Io.tcp_write(fd, ((buf as size_t) + done) as pointer[void], count - done)
            if r <= 0
                return false
            set done: done + (r as size_t)
        }
        true
    }

    ; Write the WHOLE buffer, looping over partial writes (milestone
    ; 2.5 — Console goes through here). Colorless: suspends inside a
    ; task on a nonblocking fd, drives the dispatcher from the main
    ; context, plain-blocks without a scheduler. False on a hard error.
    procedure write_all(fd: i32, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns bool
    {
        var done: size_t 0
        while done < count
        {
            let r Io.write(fd, ((buf as size_t) + done) as pointer[void], count - done)
            if r <= 0
                return false
            set done: done + (r as size_t)
        }
        true
    }

    ; Is the caller running inside a scheduled task? File and friends
    ; branch on this: offload inside a task, plain blocking call
    ; otherwise — whether an operation suspends is a runtime property.
    function in_task() returns bool
        current_task <> null

    ; How many blocking operations this thread shipped to a worker —
    ; the suspension proof for tests.
    function offload_count() returns i64
        io_offloads

    ; This thread's worker pool, created lazily on first offload.
    procedure ensure_pool() returns ref[IoPool]
    {
        var pl io_pool as ref[IoPool]?
        if pl = null
        {
            let p Page.allocate_page_traced("Io.pool_page")
            set pl: p.allocate(sizeof IoPool, alignof IoPool) as pointer[IoPool]
            var fds u8[8]
            let rc pipe(fds as pointer[i32])
            if rc <> 0
                exit 111
            set pl.lock: 0
            set pl.head: null
            set pl.tail: null
            set pl.pipe_read: *(fds as pointer[i32])
            set pl.pipe_write: *(((fds as size_t) + 4) as pointer[i32])
            set pl.workers: 0
            set pl.idle: 0
            set pl.handles: p.allocate((IO_POOL_WORKERS * 8) as size_t, 8) as pointer[size_t]
            set io_pool: pl as pointer[void]
        }
        pl as ref[IoPool]
    }

    ; Run fn(arg) — a blocking operation, `procedure fn(arg:
    ; pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]` taken with &fn — without
    ; stalling the scheduler (milestone 2.5). Inside a task the call is
    ; shipped to a worker thread and the task parks until the result
    ; arrives (the worker wakes it through the milestone-2.4 inject
    ; queue + wake handle); on the main context, or without a
    ; scheduler, fn runs right here and blocks — the colorless
    ; fallback. fn runs on ANOTHER THREAD: it must not read this
    ; thread's thread-local globals, and a non-null result must be
    ; rooted on its own fresh heap page — the caller consumes and frees
    ; it like a channel message. arg may point into the caller's
    ; region: the requesting frame outlives the call.
    procedure offload(fn: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]
    {
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        if me = null
            return scaly_call_ptr(fn, arg)
        Io.ensure_wake()
        let pl Io.ensure_pool()
        set io_offloads: io_offloads + 1
        ; The work record rides on its own heap page — freed below once
        ; the result is consumed.
        let p Page.allocate_page_traced("Io.work_page")
        let w p.allocate(sizeof IoWork, alignof IoWork) as pointer[IoWork]
        set w.next: null
        set w.fn: fn
        set w.arg: arg
        set w.result: null
        set w.task: me as ref[SchedTask]
        ; Queue the work; grow the pool when no worker is idle (up to
        ; the cap — beyond it the item waits for the next free worker).
        IoPool.acquire(pl)
        if pl.tail = null
        {
            set pl.head: w
            set pl.tail: w
        }
        else
        {
            let tl pl.tail
            set tl.next: w
            set pl.tail: w
        }
        var grow false
        if pl.idle = 0
        {
            if pl.workers < IO_POOL_WORKERS
            {
                set pl.workers: pl.workers + 1
                set grow: true
            }
        }
        IoPool.release(pl)
        if grow
        {
            let h Thread.spawn(&io_worker, pl as pointer[void])
            let idx pl.workers - 1
            set *(((pl.handles as size_t) + ((idx * 8) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t]): h
        }
        ; One byte per item: an idle worker's blocking read consumes
        ; exactly one and pops exactly one.
        var one u8[1]
        set *(one as pointer[u8]): 1
        scaly_eio_write(pl.pipe_write, one as pointer[void], 1 as size_t)
        ; Park like a channel receive: blocked + chan_parked keep
        ; step_or_wait blocking in the poller until the worker's inject
        ; arrives (drain_inject undoes both counters).
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        set s.blocked: s.blocked + 1
        set s.chan_parked: s.chan_parked + 1
        Fiber.yield()
        ; Woken: the worker published the result before the inject.
        let res w.result
        let wp Page.get(w as pointer[void])
        wp.deallocate_extensions()
        Page.release_page_traced(wp, "Io.work_page")
        res
    }

    ; Live workers of this thread's pool (0 after drain_pool) — the
    ; reclamation proof for tests.
    function pool_worker_count() returns i64
    {
        let pl io_pool as ref[IoPool]?
        if pl = null
            return 0 as i64
        pl.workers
    }

    ; Shut down this thread's worker pool (milestone 2.5 residual):
    ; closing the pipe's write end hands every idle worker's 1-byte
    ; read a 0 — its loop exits and the thread ends (an in-flight
    ; operation finishes first; its wake was already routed). Join the
    ; workers (each reclaims its own allocator state through the spawn
    ; trampoline), close the read end, free the pool page. Runs
    ; automatically at thread exit (scaly_thread_start) and is callable
    ; any time — the next offload builds a fresh pool. Must not run
    ; while a task is parked on an offloaded operation.
    procedure drain_pool()
    {
        let pl io_pool as ref[IoPool]?
        if pl = null
            return
        close(pl.pipe_write as int)
        var i: i64 0
        while i < pl.workers
        {
            let h *(((pl.handles as size_t) + ((i * 8) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t])
            Thread.join(h)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        close(pl.pipe_read as int)
        let pp Page.get(pl as pointer[void])
        pp.deallocate_extensions()
        Page.release_page_traced(pp, "Io.pool_page")
        set io_pool: null
    }

    ; Close this thread's poller and wake handle (milestone 2.5
    ; residual), read from the TLS fd mirrors — NEVER from the
    ; Scheduler record, which may sit on a page the thread entry
    ; already released. The wake close goes through the shim: whether
    ; the handle is a real fd is OS-specific (an eventfd on linux, a
    ; kqueue ident on darwin). Runs automatically at thread exit —
    ; end-of-scheduler-life only: a still-live scheduler would keep
    ; polling its (now closed) fd and trap. Sequential schedulers on
    ; one thread overwrite the mirror; the earlier poller's fds are
    ; the documented leak of that rare pattern.
    procedure close_poller()
    {
        let q io_poller_fd
        if q = 0
            return
        let w io_wake_fd
        if w <> 0
        {
            scaly_eio_wake_close((q - 1) as i32, (w - 1) as i32)
            set io_wake_fd: 0
        }
        ; Through the shim, for the reason the comment above gives about the
        ; WAKE handle — which holds for the queue itself just as much and was
        ; overlooked here: on Windows the queue is an IOCP HANDLE and POSIX
        ; close(2) on it kills the process.
        scaly_eio_close((q - 1) as i32)
        set io_poller_fd: 0
    }
}

; A channel node: one queued message. Allocated on the MESSAGE'S OWN page
; (the sender owns the message until the send, so the bump allocation is
; race-free) and freed with the message region by the receiver — the
; queue needs no allocator of its own and nothing to track.
define ChannelNode
(
    next: ref[ChannelNode]?
    msg: pointer[void]
)

; The channel — an ASYNC ownership channel connecting fibers and
; threads alike (milestone 2.4; THE channel type since the post-stage-3
; unification, which retired the single-thread rendezvous Channel of
; milestone 1.4 in its favor). null is RESERVED as the closed signal,
; so a message must be non-null — end-of-stream is close_sender, not a
; sentinel message. Capacity is a choice: an UNBOUNDED channel
; (create) never blocks a send — the message is an owned region,
; enqueue and move on; a BOUNDED channel (create_bounded(capacity))
; applies BACKPRESSURE — a send whose queue is full blocks until a
; receiver frees a slot. Deadlock avoidance stays topology-based: keep
; the channel graph acyclic along the spawn tree (discipline for now —
; a structural topology check is still deferred) and no wait cycle can
; form; a single-thread cycle is still caught by exit 106, a cross-
; thread one is not. Sender lifetime is explicit: create hands the
; creator ONE send handle, add_sender registers another BEFORE it is
; handed over, close_sender retires one — when the last retires the
; channel is CLOSED and every parked receiver wakes with null (after
; the queue drains: close is a termination signal, not a discard).
; Ownership moves with the send: message = object graph rooted on its
; own heap page, the receiver frees it.
;
; Threading contract (v1): a sender on an UNBOUNDED channel may live on
; ANY thread and needs no scheduler (its sends never park); a sender on
; a BOUNDED channel that actually blocks needs a scheduler context to
; park in (a scheduler-less bounded sender falls back to sleep-polling).
; All receivers of one channel live on ONE thread, which needs a
; scheduler; receive parks the running task (or drives the dispatcher
; from the main context) and a remote sender wakes the receiver through
; its home scheduler's inject queue + wake handle. exit 106 keeps its
; meaning unchanged for programs without channel parks.
;
;   let m Channel.create()            ; unbounded; on its own heap page; 1 handle
;   let m Channel.create_bounded(64)  ; bounded; a full send blocks
;   Channel.add_sender(m)             ; before handing m to another sender
;   Channel.send(m, msg)              ; msg non-null, page-rooted; may block if bounded
;   Channel.close_sender(m)           ; last close → receivers wake with null
;   let msg Channel.receive(m)        ; null = closed and drained
;   Channel.dispose(m)                ; receiver, after null: free the record
define Channel
(
    lock: u64                        ; spinlock (bucket_lock pattern)
    senders: i64                     ; live send handles; 0 = closed
    closed: bool
    head: ref[ChannelNode]?          ; queued messages, FIFO
    tail: ref[ChannelNode]?
    recv_head: ref[SchedTask]?    ; parked receivers, FIFO
    recv_tail: ref[SchedTask]?
    ; recv_sched: the receiving thread's scheduler — where a
    ; queued-node send pokes the wake handle.
    recv_sched: pointer[void]
    ; Bounded-capacity backpressure. capacity = 0 means UNBOUNDED (a
    ; send never blocks — the original v1 behavior); capacity > 0 caps
    ; the queue at that many undelivered messages. count is the current
    ; queue depth. send_head/send_tail is the FIFO of senders parked
    ; because the queue was full — a receiver wakes one per freed slot,
    ; the woken sender re-checks and enqueues (immutable capacity is
    ; read without the lock; count and the FIFO are lock-guarded).
    capacity: i64
    count: i64
    send_head: ref[SchedTask]?    ; parked senders (full queue), FIFO
    send_tail: ref[SchedTask]?
)
{
    ; The channel spinlock — same CAS pattern as root_pages bucket_lock:
    ; critical sections are a few pointer moves, one lock at a time.
    procedure acquire(m: pointer[Channel])
    {
        let lp &m.lock
        repeat
        {
            if scaly_atomic_cas_u64(lp, 0 as u64, 1 as u64) = (0 as u64)
                break
        }
    }

    procedure release(m: pointer[Channel])
    {
        let lp &m.lock
        scaly_atomic_store_u64(lp, 0 as u64)
    }

    ; Create an UNBOUNDED channel on its OWN heap page (like a message:
    ; the record is shared cross-thread and must not sit in any fiber's
    ; or scheduler's region). Sends never block. The creator holds the
    ; first send handle.
    procedure create() returns ref[Channel]
    {
        Channel.create_bounded(0)
    }

    ; Create a channel with a bounded queue: at most `capacity`
    ; undelivered messages, after which a send BLOCKS until a receiver
    ; frees a slot (backpressure). capacity 0 is unbounded (== create).
    procedure create_bounded(capacity: i64) returns ref[Channel]
    {
        let p Page.allocate_page_traced("Channel.record_page")
        let m p.allocate(sizeof Channel, alignof Channel) as pointer[Channel]
        set m.lock: 0
        set m.senders: 1
        set m.closed: false
        set m.head: null
        set m.tail: null
        set m.recv_head: null
        set m.recv_tail: null
        set m.recv_sched: null
        set m.capacity: capacity
        set m.count: 0
        set m.send_head: null
        set m.send_tail: null
        m
    }

    ; Free the channel record's page. Only the receiver calls this,
    ; after receive returned null (closed and drained) — every sender
    ; retired its handle, nothing can touch the record anymore.
    procedure dispose(m: ref[Channel])
    {
        let mp Page.get(m as pointer[void])
        mp.deallocate_extensions()
        Page.release_page_traced(mp, "Channel.record_page")
    }

    ; Register one more send handle. Call BEFORE handing the channel to
    ; the new sender (spawn argument, cell, ...) — registering from the
    ; new thread would race the creator's close_sender.
    procedure add_sender(m: pointer[Channel])
    {
        Channel.acquire(m)
        set m.senders: m.senders + 1
        Channel.release(m)
    }

    ; Hand a woken receiver back to its dispatcher: same thread →
    ; straight to the ready queue (the counters are ours); other thread
    ; → inject queue + wake (its drain_inject adjusts the counters).
    procedure route(r: ref[SchedTask])
    {
        deadlock_event()
        if r.home = current_scheduler
        {
            let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
            Scheduler.enqueue(r)
            set s.blocked: s.blocked - 1
            set s.chan_parked: s.chan_parked - 1
            return
        }
        Scheduler.inject_task(r.home as pointer[Scheduler], r)
    }

    procedure pop_receiver(m: ref[Channel]) returns ref[SchedTask]?
    {
        let t m.recv_head
        if t <> null
        {
            set m.recv_head: t.next
            if m.recv_head = null
                set m.recv_tail: null
            set t.next: null
        }
        t
    }

    ; Pop the oldest sender parked on a full queue (null when none).
    procedure pop_sender(m: ref[Channel]) returns ref[SchedTask]?
    {
        let t m.send_head
        if t <> null
        {
            set m.send_head: t.next
            if m.send_head = null
                set m.send_tail: null
            set t.next: null
        }
        t
    }

    ; Send msg through the channel. OWNERSHIP OF THE MESSAGE REGION MOVES
    ; TO THE RECEIVER — the sender must not touch msg afterwards. msg
    ; must be non-null (null is the closed signal) and rooted on its own
    ; heap page (Page.allocate_page — the node rides on it). Sending on a
    ; closed channel is a handle-discipline bug and traps (exit 110).
    ;
    ; On an UNBOUNDED channel (capacity 0) the node is queued and the
    ; sender moves on — never blocks, no scheduler needed. On a BOUNDED
    ; channel a send whose queue is already full BLOCKS until a receiver
    ; frees a slot: a task parks on the sender FIFO (woken by the next
    ; receive) and re-checks; the main context / a scheduler-less sender
    ; drives any ready local work and otherwise sleep-polls. Single-
    ; thread send cycles stay caught by exit 106 (the parked sender
    ; counts as blocked); cross-thread cycles remain the acyclic-topology
    ; discipline's responsibility, exactly as for receives.
    procedure send(m: pointer[Channel], msg: pointer[void])
    {
        let host Page.get(msg)
        let n host.allocate(sizeof ChannelNode, alignof ChannelNode) as pointer[ChannelNode]
        set n.next: null
        set n.msg: msg
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        ; A bounded task-sender may park and be woken cross-thread — arm
        ; the wake handle once, before any lock (ensure_wake syscalls and
        ; needs the scheduler). Unbounded and scheduler-less senders skip
        ; it: they never park on the FIFO.
        if me <> null
            if m.capacity > 0
                Io.ensure_wake()
        repeat
        {
            Channel.acquire(m)
            if m.closed
            {
                Channel.release(m)
                exit 110
            }
            var room true
            if m.capacity > 0
                if m.count >= m.capacity
                    set room: false
            if room
            {
                if m.tail = null
                {
                    set m.head: n
                    set m.tail: n
                }
                else
                {
                    let tl m.tail
                    set tl.next: n
                    set m.tail: n
                }
                set m.count: m.count + 1
                ; A queued message lets a waiting receiver progress —
                ; note the event so a concurrent deadlock check re-arms.
                deadlock_event()
                let r Channel.pop_receiver(m)
                let rs m.recv_sched
                Channel.release(m)
                if r <> null
                {
                    Channel.route(r)
                    return
                }
                ; No parked receiver — poke a possible main-context
                ; waiter on the receiving thread (spurious pokes are
                ; harmless: waiters re-check the queue on every wake).
                if rs <> null
                {
                    if rs <> current_scheduler
                        Scheduler.wake_remote(rs as pointer[Scheduler])
                }
                return
            }
            ; Full: back-pressure until a receiver frees a slot.
            if me = null
            {
                ; Main context or no scheduler: make local progress if we
                ; can, else sleep-poll a millisecond and re-check. Never
                ; block on the wake handle here — the freeing receiver may
                ; be on another thread and pokes nothing on the send side.
                Channel.release(m)
                var ran false
                if current_scheduler <> null
                    set ran: Scheduler.step()
                if not ran
                    poll(null as pointer[void], 0 as u64, 1 as i32)
            }
            else
            {
                ; Task: park on the sender FIFO and yield. A receive that
                ; frees a slot pops and routes us; we re-check on wake.
                set me.next: null
                if m.send_tail = null
                {
                    set m.send_head: me
                    set m.send_tail: me
                }
                else
                {
                    let tl m.send_tail
                    set tl.next: me
                    set m.send_tail: me
                }
                Channel.release(m)
                let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
                set s.blocked: s.blocked + 1
                set s.chan_parked: s.chan_parked + 1
                Fiber.yield()
            }
        }
    }

    ; Retire one send handle. The LAST retirement closes the channel:
    ; every parked receiver is woken (it finds the queue, then null) and
    ; a main-context waiter on the receiving thread is poked. Wakes are
    ; routed after the lock drops — inject/wake need no lock, and the
    ; receivers were detached atomically.
    procedure close_sender(m: pointer[Channel])
    {
        Channel.acquire(m)
        set m.senders: m.senders - 1
        if m.senders > 0
        {
            Channel.release(m)
            return
        }
        set m.closed: true
        ; Close wakes every parked receiver (with null) — note the event.
        deadlock_event()
        var chain m.recv_head
        set m.recv_head: null
        set m.recv_tail: null
        ; Parked senders cannot coexist with a close under correct handle
        ; discipline (a parked sender still holds its send handle, so
        ; senders > 0). Detach any anyway — a woken sender re-checks,
        ; sees closed, and traps exit 110 instead of hanging on misuse.
        var schain m.send_head
        set m.send_head: null
        set m.send_tail: null
        let rs m.recv_sched
        Channel.release(m)
        while chain <> null
        {
            let nx chain.next
            set chain.next: null
            Channel.route(chain)
            set chain: nx
        }
        while schain <> null
        {
            let nx schain.next
            set schain.next: null
            Channel.route(schain)
            set schain: nx
        }
        if rs <> null
        {
            if rs <> current_scheduler
                Scheduler.wake_remote(rs as pointer[Scheduler])
        }
    }

    ; Receive the next message; null means closed AND drained. The
    ; caller assumes ownership of the message region and frees it when
    ; done (deallocate_extensions + Page.release_page on Page.get —
    ; the queue node goes with it). Blocks until a message or the
    ; close arrives: a task parks on the receiver FIFO (woken it just
    ; re-checks the queue — the message travels in the queue, not in
    ; the task's msg slot), the main context drives the dispatcher and
    ; blocks on the wake handle when nothing is runnable. The receiving
    ; thread needs a scheduler (Scheduler.create) — the wake handle is
    ; armed here BEFORE any park so remote senders always find it.
    procedure receive(m: pointer[Channel]) returns pointer[void]
    {
        Io.ensure_wake()
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        repeat
        {
            Channel.acquire(m)
            let n m.head
            if n <> null
            {
                set m.head: n.next
                if m.head = null
                    set m.tail: null
                set m.count: m.count - 1
                ; A freed slot lets one parked sender proceed (bounded
                ; channels only — the FIFO is empty otherwise).
                let snd Channel.pop_sender(m)
                Channel.release(m)
                if snd <> null
                    Channel.route(snd)
                return n.msg
            }
            if m.closed
            {
                Channel.release(m)
                return null
            }
            set m.recv_sched: current_scheduler
            if me = null
            {
                ; Main context: run ready tasks / poll Io waiters; when
                ; nothing is runnable, block on the wake handle (a send
                ; or close from a remote thread pokes it; a poke that
                ; raced ahead completes the poll immediately).
                Channel.release(m)
                if not Scheduler.step_or_wait()
                    Scheduler.poll_io()
            }
            else
            {
                ; Task: park on the receiver FIFO. The counters are
                ; bumped after the lock drops but BEFORE the yield —
                ; a remote sender may pop us the moment the lock is
                ; released, but its inject is only drained by this
                ; thread's dispatcher, i.e. after the yield below.
                set me.next: null
                if m.recv_tail = null
                {
                    set m.recv_head: me
                    set m.recv_tail: me
                }
                else
                {
                    let tl m.recv_tail
                    set tl.next: me
                    set m.recv_tail: me
                }
                Channel.release(m)
                let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
                set s.blocked: s.blocked + 1
                set s.chan_parked: s.chan_parked + 1
                Fiber.yield()
            }
        }
    }

    ; Bounded poller wait for the timed receive: one wake-handle slice,
    ; io-task tags drained exactly as in poll_io. NO deadlock bracket —
    ; a timed waiter always has its own way out, so it must not count
    ; toward exit 106.
    procedure timed_poll(slice_ms: i32)
    {
        let s current_scheduler as ref[Scheduler]
        var tagbuf u8[512]
        let n scaly_eio_wait_timeout(s.eio, tagbuf as pointer[void], 64 as i32, slice_ms)
        if n = ((0 - 1) as i32)
            exit 107
        var i 0
        while i < (n as i64)
        {
            let raw *(((tagbuf as size_t) + ((i * 8) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t])
            ; 0 = main-context own-event tag, 1 = wake sentinel (the
            ; caller re-checks its queue on return); > 1 = an Io-parked
            ; task's record.
            if raw > 1
            {
                let t raw as pointer[SchedTask]
                Scheduler.enqueue(t)
                set s.blocked: s.blocked - 1
                set s.io_waiting: s.io_waiting - 1
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; Timed receive (stage 7, memo rule 6 — timeouts are explicit and
    ; local). Exactly receive's contract plus a deadline: a message, or
    ; null with *timed_out=false for CLOSED AND DRAINED, or null with
    ; *timed_out=true when ms milliseconds passed first. The main
    ; context drives ready work and blocks in the poller in bounded
    ; slices (a remote send/close pokes the armed wake handle, so a
    ; slice normally ends early); overshoot is at most one slice
    ; (CHAN_DEADLOCK_MS). A TASK caller cooperatively yields between
    ; checks (v1: the runtime has no timer wheel — sibling tasks run in
    ; between, an otherwise-idle scheduler spins until the deadline).
    procedure receive_timeout(m: pointer[Channel], ms: i32, timed_out: pointer[bool]) returns pointer[void]
    {
        set *timed_out: false
        Io.ensure_wake()
        let me current_task as ref[SchedTask]?
        let deadline scaly_eio_now_ns() + ((ms as i64) * (1000000 as i64))
        repeat
        {
            Channel.acquire(m)
            let n m.head
            if n <> null
            {
                set m.head: n.next
                if m.head = null
                    set m.tail: null
                set m.count: m.count - 1
                let snd Channel.pop_sender(m)
                Channel.release(m)
                if snd <> null
                    Channel.route(snd)
                return n.msg
            }
            if m.closed
            {
                Channel.release(m)
                return null
            }
            set m.recv_sched: current_scheduler
            Channel.release(m)
            let remaining deadline - scaly_eio_now_ns()
            if remaining <= 0
            {
                set *timed_out: true
                return null
            }
            if me = null
            {
                if not Scheduler.step()
                {
                    var slice (remaining + 999999) / 1000000
                    if slice > (CHAN_DEADLOCK_MS as i64)
                        set slice: CHAN_DEADLOCK_MS as i64
                    if slice < 1
                        set slice: 1
                    Channel.timed_poll(slice as i32)
                }
            }
            else
                Scheduler.yield()
        }
    }
}

; An OS thread (milestone 2.1) — the raw primitive under everything in
; stage 2. The spawned entry MUST NOT ALLOCATE until milestone 2.2 makes
; the allocator globals thread-local: see the no-allocation convention
; in the module header.
define Thread
{
    ; Run entry (a `procedure (arg: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]`,
    ; taken with &entry) on a new OS thread; arg is handed to it verbatim.
    ; Returns the thread handle for Thread.join. Traps (exit 109) when
    ; the OS refuses a thread. The thread runs through
    ; scaly_thread_start, which reclaims the thread's allocator buckets
    ; after the entry returns (milestone 2.3b) — the spawn cell rides on
    ; its own heap page because the spawner may return before the thread
    ; starts; the trampoline releases it.
    procedure spawn(fn: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void]) returns size_t
    {
        let p Page.allocate_page_traced("Thread.spawn_cell")
        let cell p.allocate(16, 8) as pointer[size_t]
        set *cell: fn as size_t
        let slot1 (cell as size_t + 8) as pointer[size_t]
        set *slot1: arg as size_t
        ; Count the new thread in the deadlock census BEFORE it starts, so
        ; it is a known potential sender before it can do anything (the
        ; trampoline drops the count at exit). Single-threaded on the
        ; first spawn — the state is created here if needed.
        ensure_deadlock_state()
        let d deadlock_state
        let tp &d.threads
        scaly_atomic_add_u64(tp, 1 as u64)
        var handle u8[8]
        let hp handle as pointer[size_t]
        let rc pthread_create(hp, null, &scaly_thread_start, cell as pointer[void])
        if rc <> 0
            exit 109
        *hp
    }

    ; Wait until the thread's entry returned (its result is discarded).
    procedure join(t: size_t)
    {
        let rc pthread_join(t, null)
        if rc <> 0
            exit 109
    }
}

; A completion group (milestone 3.2): counts a wave of submitted tasks
; and lets the submitter block until every one finished. The record
; rides its OWN heap page — work items are bump-allocated on it by the
; submitter (single submitting thread per group; workers only read
; them) and the whole page dies in dispose. The pipe is the completion
; wake: the finish that takes pending to zero writes one byte; wait
; re-checks the counter after every byte, so stale bytes from earlier
; zero-crossings (waves reusing one group) are harmless. Reusable:
; submit a wave, wait, submit the next.
define TaskGroup
(
    pending: u64                 ; in-flight tasks (atomic)
    active: u64                  ; finishers inside finish_one (atomic;
                                 ; the dispose barrier — see below)
    pipe_read: i32               ; wait blocks reading here
    pipe_write: i32              ; last completion writes one byte
)
{
    ; Create a group on its own heap page. Traps (exit 112) when the
    ; OS refuses a pipe.
    procedure create() returns ref[TaskGroup]
    {
        let p Page.allocate_page_traced("TaskGroup.record_page")
        let g p.allocate(sizeof TaskGroup, alignof TaskGroup) as pointer[TaskGroup]
        set g.pending: 0
        set g.active: 0
        var fds u8[8]
        let rc pipe(fds as pointer[i32])
        if rc <> 0
            exit 112
        set g.pipe_read: *(fds as pointer[i32])
        set g.pipe_write: *(((fds as size_t) + 4) as pointer[i32])
        g
    }

    ; One task finished: drop the pending count; the drop to zero wakes
    ; the waiter. Runs on worker threads — the counter is the only
    ; shared word, the pipe write is atomic for one byte. The active
    ; enter/leave brackets the decrement AND the wake write: a finisher
    ; descheduled between them must hold dispose open, or wait (which
    ; checks the counter BEFORE reading) returns on the bare zero, the
    ; pipe closes, and the late byte lands in a dead — or, after fd
    ; reuse, a foreign — descriptor (SIGPIPE killed tiny-wave promoted
    ; loops, 2026-07-10).
    procedure finish_one(g: pointer[TaskGroup])
    {
        let ap &g.active
        let prev scaly_atomic_add_u64(ap, 1 as u64)
        let pp &g.pending
        let old scaly_atomic_add_u64(pp, (0 - 1) as u64)
        if old = 1
        {
            var one u8[1]
            set *(one as pointer[u8]): 1
            scaly_eio_write(g.pipe_write, one as pointer[void], 1 as size_t)
        }
        let fini scaly_atomic_add_u64(ap, (0 - 1) as u64)
    }

    ; Block until every submitted task of the wave finished. Call from
    ; the SUBMITTING thread, after all submits (a submit racing a wait
    ; could let a stale zero-crossing byte end the wait early). Blocks
    ; the whole thread — main-context use, not inside a scheduled task.
    procedure wait(g: pointer[TaskGroup])
    {
        let pp &g.pending
        while scaly_atomic_load_u64(pp) > (0 as u64)
        {
            var byte u8[1]
            let n scaly_eio_read(g.pipe_read, byte as pointer[void], 1 as size_t)
            if n <> 1
                exit 112
        }
    }

    ; Free the group: close the pipe, release the record page (every
    ; work item of every wave goes with it). Only after wait returned —
    ; nothing may touch the record or its items anymore. wait can
    ; return on the counter while the last finisher still owes its wake
    ; byte (it checks pending BEFORE reading), so spin the finish_one
    ; bracket empty before closing: pending is zero and no submits
    ; follow, hence no NEW finisher can enter — the spin is bounded by
    ; one descheduled write.
    procedure dispose(g: pointer[TaskGroup])
    {
        let ap &g.active
        repeat
        {
            if scaly_atomic_load_u64(ap) = (0 as u64)
                break
        }
        close(g.pipe_write as int)
        close(g.pipe_read as int)
        let gp Page.get(g as pointer[void])
        gp.deallocate_extensions()
        Page.release_page_traced(gp, "TaskGroup.record_page")
    }
}

; One submitted task (milestone 3.2). Lives on its group's page (the
; submitter allocates, workers only read, dispose frees wholesale).
define TaskItem
(
    next: ref[TaskItem]?
    fn: pointer[void]            ; procedure fn(arg) returns pointer[void]
    arg: pointer[void]
    group: ref[TaskGroup]
)

; One worker's work deque (milestone 3.2): a spinlocked FIFO — the
; submitter appends round-robin, the owner pops, thieves pop too. The
; lock is the whole synchronization story (bucket_lock pattern:
; critical sections are a few pointer moves); the stealing is in the
; DISTRIBUTION — N deques instead of one shared queue keep submitters
; and workers off each other's locks until a worker actually runs dry.
define TaskDeque
(
    lock: u64                    ; spinlock (bucket_lock pattern)
    head: ref[TaskItem]?
    tail: ref[TaskItem]?
)
{
    procedure acquire(d: pointer[TaskDeque])
    {
        let lp &d.lock
        repeat
        {
            if scaly_atomic_cas_u64(lp, 0 as u64, 1 as u64) = (0 as u64)
                break
        }
    }

    procedure release(d: pointer[TaskDeque])
    {
        let lp &d.lock
        scaly_atomic_store_u64(lp, 0 as u64)
    }
}

; The work-stealing pool (milestone 3.2) — see the module header for
; the design and the contracts. The record, the deques, the worker
; spawn cells, and the join handles all ride the pool's own heap page;
; workers reach everything through their spawn cell (a worker thread
; never sees the creator's thread-local globals — the milestone-2.4
; lesson).
define TaskPool
(
    deques: ref[TaskDeque]   ; nworkers records, on the pool page
    nworkers: i64
    next_deque: i64              ; round-robin submit cursor (a plain
                                 ; load-balancing hint: racy submitters
                                 ; only skew the distribution)
    stolen: u64                  ; items taken from a foreign deque
                                 ; (atomic; observability for tests)
    submitted: u64               ; items ever submitted (atomic;
                                 ; observability — 4.1 chunk collapse)
    pipe_read: i32               ; workers block reading here
    pipe_write: i32              ; one byte written per submitted item
    handles: pointer[size_t]     ; worker join handles (creator-written)
)
{
    ; The idx-th worker's deque.
    function deque_at(pl: ref[TaskPool], idx: i64) returns ref[TaskDeque]
        ((pl.deques as size_t) + ((idx as size_t) * sizeof TaskDeque)) as pointer[TaskDeque]

    ; How many items workers stole from a foreign deque — observability
    ; for tests (the count is timing-dependent, assert nothing strict).
    function steal_count(pl: pointer[TaskPool]) returns u64
    {
        let sp &pl.stolen
        scaly_atomic_load_u64(sp)
    }

    ; How many items were ever submitted — observability for the 4.1
    ; chunked emission (a chunked wave submits ~ncpu, not n).
    function submit_count(pl: pointer[TaskPool]) returns u64
    {
        let sp &pl.submitted
        scaly_atomic_load_u64(sp)
    }

    ; Start a pool of nworkers OS threads (clamped to at least one).
    ; Traps (exit 112) when the OS refuses the pipe; a refused thread
    ; traps in Thread.spawn (exit 109).
    procedure create(nworkers: i64) returns ref[TaskPool]
    {
        var n nworkers
        if n < 1
            set n: 1
        let p Page.allocate_page_traced("TaskPool.pool_page")
        let pl p.allocate(sizeof TaskPool, alignof TaskPool) as pointer[TaskPool]
        set pl.nworkers: n
        set pl.next_deque: 0
        set pl.stolen: 0
        set pl.submitted: 0
        var fds u8[8]
        let rc pipe(fds as pointer[i32])
        if rc <> 0
            exit 112
        set pl.pipe_read: *(fds as pointer[i32])
        set pl.pipe_write: *(((fds as size_t) + 4) as pointer[i32])
        set pl.deques: p.allocate((n as size_t) * sizeof TaskDeque, alignof TaskDeque) as pointer[TaskDeque]
        set pl.handles: p.allocate((n * 8) as size_t, 8) as pointer[size_t]
        var i: i64 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d TaskPool.deque_at(pl, i)
            set d.lock: 0
            set d.head: null
            set d.tail: null
            set i: i + 1
        }
        ; Worker spawn cells {pool, index}, 16 bytes each on the pool
        ; page — the worker's only door to the pool.
        let cells p.allocate((n * 16) as size_t, 8) as pointer[size_t]
        set i: 0
        while i < n
        {
            let c ((cells as size_t) + ((i * 16) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t]
            set *c: pl as size_t
            set *(((c as size_t) + 8) as pointer[size_t]): i as size_t
            let h Thread.spawn(&task_worker, c as pointer[void])
            set *(((pl.handles as size_t) + ((i * 8) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t]): h
            set i: i + 1
        }
        pl
    }

    ; Submit fn(arg) — a task entry, `procedure fn(arg: pointer[void])
    ; returns pointer[void]` taken with &fn — into the pool under the
    ; given group. Never blocks. The item rides the group's page (one
    ; submitting thread per group); the pending increment comes FIRST
    ; (a task may finish before submit returns), the deque push BEFORE
    ; the pipe byte (a woken worker must find an item).
    procedure submit(pl: pointer[TaskPool], fn: pointer[void], arg: pointer[void], g: pointer[TaskGroup])
    {
        let sc &pl.submitted
        scaly_atomic_add_u64(sc, 1 as u64)
        let pp &g.pending
        let old scaly_atomic_add_u64(pp, 1 as u64)
        let host Page.get(g as pointer[void])
        let w host.allocate(sizeof TaskItem, alignof TaskItem) as pointer[TaskItem]
        set w.next: null
        set w.fn: fn
        set w.arg: arg
        set w.group: g
        var idx pl.next_deque
        if idx >= pl.nworkers
            set idx: 0
        set pl.next_deque: idx + 1
        let d TaskPool.deque_at(pl, idx)
        TaskDeque.acquire(d)
        if d.tail = null
        {
            set d.head: w
            set d.tail: w
        }
        else
        {
            let tl d.tail
            set tl.next: w
            set d.tail: w
        }
        TaskDeque.release(d)
        var one u8[1]
        set *(one as pointer[u8]): 1
        scaly_eio_write(pl.pipe_write, one as pointer[void], 1 as size_t)
    }

    ; Take one item: own deque first, then steal round-robin from the
    ; others. Only called after a pipe byte was consumed, so at least
    ; one item exists SOMEWHERE — a scan pass can still race a push in
    ; flight, hence the outer retry loop (bounded by that push's
    ; completion).
    procedure take(pl: pointer[TaskPool], start: i64) returns ref[TaskItem]
    {
        repeat
        {
            var k: i64 0
            while k < pl.nworkers
            {
                var idx start + k
                if idx >= pl.nworkers
                    set idx: idx - pl.nworkers
                let d TaskPool.deque_at(pl, idx)
                TaskDeque.acquire(d)
                var w d.head
                if w <> null
                {
                    set d.head: w.next
                    if d.head = null
                        set d.tail: null
                    set w.next: null
                }
                TaskDeque.release(d)
                if w <> null
                {
                    if k > 0
                    {
                        let sp &pl.stolen
                        let old scaly_atomic_add_u64(sp, 1 as u64)
                    }
                    return w as ref[TaskItem]
                }
                set k: k + 1
            }
        }
    }

    ; Shut the pool down: closing the pipe's write end lets the workers
    ; drain every remaining byte (queued work still runs), then hands
    ; each blocked read a 0 — the worker exits and is joined (each
    ; reclaims its own allocator state through the spawn trampoline).
    ; Only when no group has work in flight that another thread still
    ; waits on; the pool is gone afterwards — create a new one to
    ; continue.
    procedure shutdown(pl: ref[TaskPool])
    {
        close(pl.pipe_write as int)
        var i: i64 0
        while i < pl.nworkers
        {
            let h *(((pl.handles as size_t) + ((i * 8) as size_t)) as pointer[size_t])
            Thread.join(h)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        close(pl.pipe_read as int)
        let pp Page.get(pl as pointer[void])
        pp.deallocate_extensions()
        Page.release_page_traced(pp, "TaskPool.pool_page")
    }

    ; Online CPU count — the worker default for default_pool (3.3).
    function ncpu() returns i64
        scaly_eio_ncpu() as i64

    ; This thread's default pool, created on first demand with ncpu
    ; workers — the dispatch target of the promoted-for emission.
    ; Reached through the TLS cell task_default_pool (never handed to
    ; another thread's TLS — workers reach the pool via their spawn
    ; cells like any pool).
    procedure default_pool() returns ref[TaskPool]
    {
        if task_default_pool <> null
            return task_default_pool as ref[TaskPool]
        let pl TaskPool.create(TaskPool.ncpu())
        set task_default_pool: pl as pointer[void]
        pl
    }

    ; Shut this thread's default pool down if one was created. Called by
    ; the thread-exit trampoline BEFORE allocator reclaim (shutdown joins
    ; the workers and frees the pool page). Safe there: every `parallel
    ; for` waits on its group before continuing, so nothing is in flight
    ; when the thread's entry has returned.
    procedure drain_default()
    {
        if task_default_pool <> null
        {
            let pl task_default_pool as ref[TaskPool]
            set task_default_pool: null
            TaskPool.shutdown(pl)
        }
    }
}

; A pool worker (milestone 3.2): consume one pipe byte (blocks between
; items; 0 = shutdown), take the item it vouches for (own deque first,
; then steal), run it, count it done. The task's allocations root on
; THIS thread's allocator and unwind with the entry frame — one region
; per task; results the task keeps go into caller-owned disjoint slots
; (see the module header). Runs on its own OS thread: everything it
; touches arrives through the spawn cell.
procedure task_worker(arg: pointer[void]) returns pointer[void]
{
    let cell arg as pointer[size_t]
    let pl (*cell) as pointer[TaskPool]
    let me ((*(((cell as size_t) + 8) as pointer[size_t])) as i64)
    repeat
    {
        var byte u8[1]
        let n scaly_eio_read(pl.pipe_read, byte as pointer[void], 1 as size_t)
        if n <> 1
            return null
        let w TaskPool.take(pl, me)
        let res scaly_call_ptr(w.fn, w.arg)
        TaskGroup.finish_one(w.group)
    }
}
